export const BLOG_FILES = [
  { slug: "agent-that-measures", locale: "en", file: "agent-that-measures.en.md", raw: "---\ntitle: A CAD agent that measures its own work\ndescription: A language model cannot see in 3D; it only knows the code ran. We give it feedback it can read, a dimension report, orthographic views, throwaway probes, a knob test. What each one answers.\ndate: 2026-08-12\n---\n\nA language model writing CAD code has one fundamental handicap: it cannot see the result. If the code ran, it tends to assume the part is right. A hole that belongs at x = 40 and got written at 35 builds fine, reports no error, and looks plausible on screen.\n\nSo we put one sentence in the agent's instructions about what \"build succeeded\" means: **a clean build means it compiles, not that it is right.** The rest is giving it feedback it can read, so that it notices 35 is not 40 by itself.\n\n## The build report: a fingerprint of the solid\n\nAfter every edit the agent has to rebuild, and what comes back is not the word \"success\" but a report:\n\n- bounding box and volume;\n- every cylindrical face, its radius, **and where its axis runs**;\n- every planar face, **at which coordinate it sits**;\n- whether the part came apart into several solids;\n- diagnostics.\n\nThe report is a fingerprint, not a verdict. The agent has to read it against the request. A Ø13 through-hole was asked for at x = 40; the report says `Ø13 @ x 35`; wrong place. A 4 mm step was asked for; the planar-face line should contain two coordinates 4 apart; it does not, so the step was never cut. An extra line saying \"disjoint solids\" means a fillet or a cut severed a boss.\n\nEvery diagnostic is taken seriously. `fillet-capped` says a fillet helper delivered less than you asked for, because the radius does not fit in that corner; the fix is to reshape the neighbourhood, not to accept the capped number. `body-interference` means two bodies share volume; a real defect, not noise, fixed by changing the gap or the placement, never by raising a tolerance. Touching is fine and does not appear.\n\n## Snapshots: look before you measure\n\nOnce the report is clean, the next step is to look. `snapshot` renders a 2 × 2 sheet: top, isometric, front, right. The three orthographic views share one scale and one centre, so a feature can be matched across them.\n\nA shaded render only draws visible outlines, so you never conclude from one that a hole is missing. For holding a part against an engineering drawing there is a line-art x-ray mode: edges hidden behind material are drawn dashed, the way a drawing does it, so a cavity, a wall thickness, a bore that stops short all show up, one projection at a time.\n\n## Probes: write a snippet for what the report cannot answer\n\nSome questions the report cannot answer: whether a seam really closed, how thick a wall is, whether a fillet reached the edge it was meant for. The agent can write a short snippet that runs appended to the model, with every top-level name in scope plus a `measure` library.\n\n```python\ndef test_bore_position():\n    bores = measure.cylinders(model, radius=6.5)\n    assert len(bores) == 1\n    assert abs(bores[0].at(\"X\") - 40) < 0.01\n\ndef test_wall():\n    walls = measure.walls(model)\n    assert min(w.thickness for w in walls) >= 1.6\n```\n\nEach `test_*` runs in isolation, so one wrong API guess fails that check and the rest still run. The assertions are against the built geometry; asserting a constant against itself proves nothing.\n\nOne rule deserves its own line: **measure with `measure`, never with hand-filtered `.faces()`.** One condition too loose and an unrelated face averages into the number; the result is wrong but plausible, which is worse than an error.\n\nAnother: once the same step has failed twice, do not rebuild the whole model a third time. Isolate that step, print what it actually produces, edge count, area, volume, validity. One isolated probe pins the failing step; another blind rebuild does not.\n\n## Knobs: push every slider to both ends\n\nConstants marked `#@` in the model become sliders and switches on the shared page. That is a promise to whoever opens the link: this slider works from here to there. `knobs` moves each one alone to its extremes and builds, and reports OK, INERT (it moved but the geometry did not, usually a number that never reaches the geometry), WARNS or BROKEN. Run it before handing a model over.\n\n## What this solves and what it does not\n\nWith all of this, simple parts often come out right the first time: a box, a bracket, a spacer, a plate with holes; the agent measures the misplacement itself and fixes it. Complex parts still take several rounds, especially assemblies that need spatial reasoning. And \"it can measure\" is not the same as \"it measures every time\"; getting it into the habit took as much work as building the tools.\n\nHow we know whether the rounds are getting better is another question, and the next article. That one is not pretty.\n\nHow much these feedback tools matter next to the choice of CAD language is in [Which CAD code should AI write?](/en/blog/ai-cad-languages)\n" },
  { slug: "agent-that-measures", locale: "zh", file: "agent-that-measures.zh.md", raw: "---\ntitle: 一个会自己量尺寸的 CAD agent\ndescription: 语言模型看不见三维，它只知道代码跑过了。我们给它一套能读的反馈：尺寸报告、三视图、临时探针、旋钮极限测试。这篇讲它们各自回答什么问题。\ndate: 2026-08-12\n---\n\n语言模型写 CAD 代码有一个根本的难处：它看不见结果。代码跑通了，它就倾向于认为对了。一个本该在 x = 40 的孔被写到了 35，构建照样成功，报告里没有报错，模型在屏幕上也像那么回事。\n\n所以「构建成功」这件事，我们在给 agent 的说明里专门写了一句：**一次干净的构建意味着它能编译，不意味着它是对的。** 剩下的就是给它一些能读的反馈，让它自己发现 35 不是 40。\n\n## 构建报告：一个固体的指纹\n\n每次改完代码，agent 必须重新构建一次，拿回来的不是「成功」两个字，是一份报告：\n\n- 包围盒和体积；\n- 每一个圆柱面，它的半径，**以及轴线在哪**；\n- 每一个平面，**它落在哪个坐标上**；\n- 有没有散成几块；\n- 诊断。\n\n这份报告不是裁判，是指纹。agent 要拿它对着需求读：要求 Ø13 通孔在 x = 40，报告里有一行 `Ø13 @ x 35`，位置错了；要求一个 4 mm 深的台阶，平面那一行里本该出现一个相差 4 的坐标，没有，就是台阶没切出来；多出一行「disjoint solids」，说明某个倒角或者切口把一个凸台切断了。\n\n诊断里的每一条我们都当真。`fillet-capped` 是说某个圆角帮手给的比你要的小，因为你要的尺寸在那个角落放不下，该做的是改周围的形状，而不是接受被削掉的数值。`body-interference` 是两个零件在同一处占了体积，这是真缺陷，不是噪声，解法是改间隙或者位置，永远不是调高容差。接触不算干涉，不会出现在这里。\n\n## 截图：先看，再量\n\n报告建干净了，下一步是看。`snapshot` 出一张 2 × 2 的图：俯视、等轴测、正视、右视，三个正交视图共享同一个比例和中心，所以同一个特征在三张图里能对上位置。\n\n着色的图只画看得见的轮廓，所以不能从一张着色图断定「孔没了」。要对照工程图的时候用线框加透视模式：隐藏在材料后面的边画成虚线，和图纸一样，一个腔、一道壁厚、一个盲孔都显出来，一个投影一个投影地比。\n\n## 探针：报告答不了的问题，自己写一段去量\n\n有些问题报告答不了：一条接缝有没有真的合上，一道壁厚到底多少，一个圆角到底切没切到那条边。agent 可以写一小段代码，附在模型后面跑，模型里所有顶层的名字都在作用域里，再加一个 `measure` 库。\n\n```python\ndef test_bore_position():\n    bores = measure.cylinders(model, radius=6.5)\n    assert len(bores) == 1\n    assert abs(bores[0].at(\"X\") - 40) < 0.01\n\ndef test_wall():\n    walls = measure.walls(model)\n    assert min(w.thickness for w in walls) >= 1.6\n```\n\n每个 `test_*` 单独跑，一个 API 猜错只让那一条失败，别的照常。断言对的是建出来的几何，对一个常量断言它自己什么也证明不了。\n\n有一条规矩值得单独说：**量尺寸用 `measure`，别自己过滤 `.faces()`。** 一个条件写松了，一个不相干的面就平均进了数字，结果错了但看起来很合理，这比直接报错糟糕得多。\n\n还有一条：同一步失败两次之后，别再整个模型重建第三次，把那一步单独拎出来，打印它真正产生的东西，边数、面积、体积、合不合法。一次孤立的探针能钉住出错的那一步，再一次盲目的整体重建不能。\n\n## 旋钮：每一个滑块推到两端\n\n模型里标了 `#@` 的常量会变成分享页上的滑块和开关。这是对打开链接的人的承诺：这个滑块从这里到那里都能动。`knobs` 把每个旋钮单独推到两个极端各建一次，结果是 OK、INERT（动了但几何没变，通常是这个数没接到几何上）、WARNS 或 BROKEN。交付前跑一次。\n\n## 它解决了什么，没解决什么\n\n有了这一套，简单件常常能一次拿到：盒子、支架、垫片、一个带孔的板，agent 自己量出错位、自己改。复杂件还是要磨几轮，尤其是需要空间想象的装配关系。而且「它能自己量」和「它每次都去量」是两回事，让它养成习惯花的功夫，不比写这些工具少。\n\n怎么知道磨得有没有进步，是另一个问题，也是下一篇。那一篇不太好看。\n\n这些反馈工具和 CAD 语言的选择相比哪个更要紧，见[《AI 写 CAD 该用哪种代码》](/zh/blog/ai-cad-languages)。\n" },
  { slug: "ai-cad-languages", locale: "en", file: "ai-cad-languages.en.md", raw: "---\ntitle: Which CAD code should AI write? build123d, CadQuery, OpenSCAD, KCL, FeatureScript and CAD APIs compared\ndescription: build123d, CadQuery, OpenSCAD, KCL, FeatureScript and the SolidWorks and Fusion APIs, compared on how well AI can write them: benchmark results, one part in six languages, and what the agent loop needs.\ndate: 2026-10-30\n---\n\nThe short answer: whether a language model writes good CAD comes down to three things. How much of that code it has seen, how the code refers to faces and edges that already exist, and whether the AI can run its own code and measure what came out. On those three, build123d and CadQuery fit best today: they are Python, they sit on a B-rep kernel, and they run headless on your own machine. On Hugging Face's CADGenBench, as of 30 October 2026, every top-10 entry that names its modelling tool uses build123d.\n\nOpenSCAD is the easiest to get running and has the lowest ceiling. KCL is the language most clearly designed with AI in mind, but models have barely seen it and its geometry is computed in Zoo's cloud. FeatureScript and the APIs of traditional CAD programs were never meant for writing a part from scratch.\n\nA person using CAD clicks and looks; the eye tells the hand which face to pick. A model only has text. When it writes \"drill a hole in the top face\", it cannot see the top face. So AI modelling is a loop: write code, run it in a kernel, render and measure the result, feed the picture and the errors back, and fix. Each section below looks at one step of that loop.\n\n![AI modelling is a loop, and the widest gap is in measuring](/blog/ai-cad-languages/loop-en.png)\n\nDisclosure: we build Forgent3D, an AI CAD platform based on build123d that also runs OpenSCAD. Why we have the AI write code rather than generate a mesh is in [Code is the model](/en/blog/code-is-model). Where our own work comes up below, we say so.\n\n## The six options at a glance\n\nOf the six, only build123d and CadQuery compute B-rep (exact geometry you can export as STEP) on your own machine, for free, without a GUI. OpenSCAD produces meshes. KCL, FeatureScript and the traditional CAD APIs all compute their geometry inside a vendor's program or cloud.\n\n| Option | Language | Kernel and output | Where it runs | Licence | Version (Oct 2026) |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| [build123d](https://github.com/gumyr/build123d) | Python library | OCCT, B-rep, exports STEP | Local `pip install`, runs headless | Apache-2.0 | 0.13.0 |\n| [CadQuery](https://github.com/CadQuery/cadquery) | Python library | OCCT, B-rep, exports STEP | Local pip or conda, runs headless | Apache-2.0 | 2.8.0 |\n| [OpenSCAD](https://openscad.org) | Its own functional language | Mesh CSG (newer builds can use the Manifold backend), exports STL and 3MF, no STEP | Desktop app and CLI, also a wasm build | GPL | Stable release plus dev snapshots |\n| [KCL](https://zoo.dev/docs/kcl-lang) | Zoo's own language | Zoo's own geometry engine, B-rep, exports STEP | Zoo Design Studio or the API; geometry is computed in Zoo's cloud | Language and client MIT, engine closed | KCL 3.0, Design Studio 1.5.3 |\n| [FeatureScript](https://cad.onshape.com/FsDoc/) | Onshape's own language | Parasolid, B-rep | Only inside Onshape | Standard library source is public, platform is commercial | Ships with Onshape |\n| Traditional CAD APIs | VBA or C# for SolidWorks, Python or C++ for Fusion, C++ or Python for NX, and so on | Each vendor's kernel (Parasolid, ShapeManager and others), B-rep | An installed, licensed desktop program; SolidWorks is Windows-only | Commercial | — |\n\nThis table already settles half the question. An AI has to write, run and check many times over, and these columns decide what each run costs, whether you can run thousands of them on a server, and whether the output is exact geometry.\n\n## What public benchmarks say\n\nThe strongest data so far is Hugging Face's [CADGenBench](https://huggingface.co/spaces/HuggingAI4Engineering/CADGenBench). As of 30 October 2026, every entry in its verified top 10 that names its modelling tool uses build123d; none uses CadQuery, OpenSCAD or KCL. First place is build123d-mcp with Claude Opus 5.5, at an overall score of 0.830.\n\nThe test is close to real work. 49 tasks give the AI an engineering drawing to model; 32 give it a STEP file and a change request (\"reduce the number of impeller blades from 7 to 5\"). The answer has to be a STEP file. It is first checked for being a valid, watertight solid, and an invalid one scores zero. Valid answers are scored on shape (40%), whether the mating features fit the same fixture (40%), and the number of pieces, through-holes and voids (20%). OpenSCAD only produces meshes, so it cannot even hand this exam in.\n\nThe language is not the whole score, though. The same model writing the same build123d can score very differently depending on its tools (chart below). The official baseline already feeds renders back to the model; [build123d-mcp](https://pypi.org/project/build123d-mcp/) adds tools to measure volume and bounding boxes, find holes, compare against the input file and validate before export. Most of the gap comes from measuring, not from looking.\n\n![With build123d-mcp, the same model scores 4 to 24 points higher on CADGenBench generation tasks](/blog/ai-cad-languages/chart-en.png)\n\nRead the chart with two caveats. The three baselines are not identical, and build123d-mcp itself kept improving over these months. Agent evaluations are also noisy: in our own, rerunning one configuration swings it by 16 points ([write-up here](/en/blog/eval-variance)). Treat +4 and +7 with care; Opus 5's +23.5 is the clear one. The scores are from the BenchLM snapshot of 28 October 2026, whose top 10 matches the live leaderboard.\n\nOther public results:\n\n| Source | Languages | What was tested | Result |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| [ModelRift blog](https://modelrift.com/blog/cadquery-vs-openscad/) (Sep 2026, vendor) | OpenSCAD, CadQuery | Claude Opus 5 in Claude Code, three printable parts | Both sides finished. OpenSCAD raised 2 errors and had 5 silent wrong-geometry cases; CadQuery had 5 and 4. CadQuery can assert dimensions in code; the OpenSCAD side had to write its own STL parser |\n| [GrandpaCAD blog](https://grandpacad.com/en/blog/openscad-vs-cadquery-vs-build123d) (vendor) | OpenSCAD, CadQuery, build123d | One model (gemini-3.5-flash), the same prompts | About 0.4 code errors per generation for OpenSCAD and 1.4 to 1.7 for build123d, mostly wrong method names and argument order |\n| [Text2CAD-Bench](https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.18430) (May 2026, paper) | CadQuery vs command sequences | 600 text prompts, seven general-purpose LLMs | CadQuery code is clearly better; with command sequences, DeepSeek-V3.2's invalid rate rose from 13.3% to 67.3% |\n| [BenchCAD](https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.10865) (May 2026, paper) | CadQuery | 17,900 industrial parts, image to code | Frontier models run but miss the shape: Claude Opus 4.7 executes 94.0% of the time with an IoU of 0.274, while a fine-tuned 2B model reaches 0.752. About 64% of \"successful\" edits silently break other features |\n| [CadQueryEval](https://github.com/danwahl/cadqueryeval) (community, archived Sep 2026) | CadQuery | 25 text tasks scored against reference STL | Four models already pass all 25; the benchmark is saturated |\n\nThe two kinds of result look contradictory: vendor blogs say OpenSCAD fails less, yet CADGenBench's leaders all use build123d. They measure different things. GrandpaCAD counts whether the code runs, on small 3D-printing parts; CADGenBench measures whether the geometry is right and whether it fits. For mechanical parts, the second is what counts.\n\nOne more caution: CADGenBench's tasks are public (the answers are not), and several rows on the board are the same author iterating on their tool. The top score is closer to a ceiling than to what you get out of the box.\n\n## How much the model has seen: training data and API churn\n\nOpenSCAD and the Python options (CadQuery, build123d) have the most training data behind them; KCL and FeatureScript the least. But training data decides whether the first draft runs, not whether the final part is right.\n\n- **OpenSCAD**: well over a decade of models on Thingiverse, Printables and GitHub, a syntax that has barely changed in years, and a few dozen keywords. Models rarely invent functions. The catch is that the corpus is mostly small printed parts; mechanical conventions are thin on the ground.\n- **CadQuery**: rides on Python, so loops, functions and maths are the parts models know best. Academic work uses it for almost every dataset; [CAD-Recode](https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.14042) alone generated a million CadQuery programs.\n- **build123d**: also Python, but younger, with less public code than CadQuery, and still at 0.x. The two share many method names, so models mix them up. That is exactly what GrandpaCAD saw: methods that don't exist, arguments in the wrong order, calls on the wrong kind of object.\n- **KCL**: two or three years old and already reworked twice. Positional arguments became keyword arguments, then KCL 3.0 in 2026 replaced sketches with constraint-solved sketch blocks, switched on with `@settings(kclVersion = 3.0)`. What a general model remembers is mostly the old syntax; it has to read the docs.\n- **FeatureScript**: public code is mostly Onshape's standard library and community custom features, a small corpus. The [FeatureScript MCP Server](https://www.onshape.com/en/blog/featurescript-mcp-server-use-cases-text-code-cad) Onshape launched in September 2026 is built around a generate, run, debug and revise loop rather than expecting the model to get it right first time.\n- **Traditional APIs**: there are plenty of SolidWorks VBA macros on forums, but most are recorded: `SelectByID2` with coordinates, `Sketch1`, `Boss-Extrude1`. What a model learns from them is how to replay someone's clicks, not how to express a design. Fusion's Python API is tidier and well documented.\n\nAPI churn costs more when an AI is writing the code. A person reads the changelog after an upgrade; a model waits for its next training run. Putting the current version's docs in front of the model, through the prompt or a tool, is work every one of these languages needs.\n\n## Referring to faces and edges: one part in six languages\n\nOne part: a 60 × 40 × 10 mm plate, R2 fillets on the four top edges, a Ø10 through-hole in the centre. The biggest difference between the six is how you say \"those four top edges\". That is where AI-written CAD goes wrong most often, because the model cannot see what it is selecting.\n\n### build123d\n\n```python\nfrom build123d import *\n\nplate = Box(60, 40, 10)\ntop_edges = plate.edges().group_by(Axis.Z)[-1]   # group by Z, take the highest group = the four top edges\nplate = fillet(top_edges, radius=2)\nplate -= Cylinder(radius=5, height=10)\n```\n\nThe selection is an ordinary Python expression: take every edge, group them by height, keep the top group. The intermediate result is a variable, so the AI can `print(len(top_edges))`, confirm it got 4, and only then carry on. We ran this on build123d 0.13.0; the volume comes out at 23,046 mm³.\n\n### CadQuery\n\n```python\nimport cadquery as cq\n\nplate = (\n    cq.Workplane(\"XY\").box(60, 40, 10)\n    .faces(\">Z\").edges().fillet(2)\n    .faces(\">Z\").workplane().hole(10)\n)\n```\n\nString selectors like `\">Z\"` are short, and models have seen plenty of them. The cost is that the whole chain shares a hidden stack: where `.workplane()` puts its origin and where `.hole()` lands depend on what the previous step left behind. In a long chain, models lose track of what is on the stack.\n\n### OpenSCAD\n\n```openscad\ninclude <BOSL2/std.scad>\n$fn = 64;\n\ndifference() {\n    cuboid([60, 40, 10], rounding = 2, edges = TOP);  // round the four top edges\n    cyl(d = 10, h = 12);                              // centre through-hole\n}\n```\n\nOpenSCAD never selects an edge. Rounding comes either from a library parameter like BOSL2's `edges = TOP`, or from building the shape yourself with `hull` and `minkowski`. There is no wrong edge to pick, but anything the library does not cover, such as rounding an edge that only appears after a boolean, is close to impossible. With the Manifold backend this renders as 474 triangles with almost the same volume as the build123d version; the rounds are flat facets.\n\n### KCL\n\n```kcl\n@settings(defaultLengthUnit = mm, kclVersion = 3.0)\n\nprofile = sketch(on = XY) {\n  front = line(start = [var -30mm, var -20mm], end = [var 30mm, var -20mm])\n  right = line(start = [var 30mm, var -20mm], end = [var 30mm, var 20mm])\n  back = line(start = [var 30mm, var 20mm], end = [var -30mm, var 20mm])\n  left = line(start = [var -30mm, var 20mm], end = [var -30mm, var -20mm])\n}\nplateRegion = region(segments = [profile.left, profile.front])\nplate = extrude(plateRegion, length = 10mm, tagEnd = $top)\n\nrounded = fillet(\n  plate,\n  edges = [\n    { sideFaces = [plateRegion.tags.front, plate.faces.top] },\n    { sideFaces = [plateRegion.tags.right, plate.faces.top] },\n    { sideFaces = [plateRegion.tags.back, plate.faces.top] },\n    { sideFaces = [plateRegion.tags.left, plate.faces.top] }\n  ],\n  radius = 2mm,\n)\n// centre hole with hole::hole(...) or subtract(...), omitted\n```\n\nKCL refers to geometry by tags: name the sketch lines, name the top face when you extrude, then describe an edge as \"the one between these two faces\". The names are chosen by whoever writes the code and do not depend on any geometric computation, which suits a model well. The cost is verbosity: four edges are written out four times, and you have to decide while sketching what you will want to refer to later. Adapted from the KCL 3.0 examples in Zoo's current docs; we have not run it.\n\n### FeatureScript\n\n```featurescript\nannotation { \"Feature Type Name\" : \"Plate\" }\nexport const plate = defineFeature(function(context is Context, id is Id, definition is map)\n    precondition {}\n    {\n        fCuboid(context, id + \"box\", {\n                \"corner1\" : vector(-30, -20, 0) * millimeter,\n                \"corner2\" : vector(30, 20, 10) * millimeter\n        });\n        // top face = the face made by \"box\" that lies on the plane z = 10\n        const top = qCoincidesWithPlane(qCreatedBy(id + \"box\", EntityType.FACE),\n                plane(vector(0, 0, 10) * millimeter, vector(0, 0, 1)));\n        opFillet(context, id + \"fillet\", { \"entities\" : top, \"radius\" : 2 * millimeter });\n        fCylinder(context, id + \"pin\", {\n                \"bottomCenter\" : vector(0, 0, 0) * millimeter,\n                \"topCenter\" : vector(0, 0, 10) * millimeter,\n                \"radius\" : 5 * millimeter\n        });\n        opBoolean(context, id + \"cut\", {\n                \"tools\" : qCreatedBy(id + \"pin\", EntityType.BODY),\n                \"targets\" : qCreatedBy(id + \"box\", EntityType.BODY),\n                \"operationType\" : BooleanOperationType.SUBTRACTION\n        });\n    }, {});\n```\n\nFeatureScript refers to geometry with queries: \"the face created by the box step that lies on the plane z = 10\". A query describes where an entity came from plus a condition, so it still finds the same face after upstream parameters change; that is why Onshape's parametrics are so stable. But the language is built for writing features: you define a feature, then use it in a Part Studio, and the ids, units and context make even a simple part twice as long. Header and imports are omitted; the signatures were checked against FsDoc.\n\n### Traditional CAD APIs (SolidWorks VBA as the example)\n\n```vb\n' SolidWorks VBA macro (excerpt). API lengths are in metres\nSet part = swApp.NewPart\npart.Extension.SelectByID2 \"Front Plane\", \"PLANE\", 0, 0, 0, False, 0, Nothing, 0\npart.SketchManager.InsertSketch True\npart.SketchManager.CreateCenterRectangle 0, 0, 0, 0.03, 0.02, 0\npart.SketchManager.CreateCircleByRadius 0, 0, 0, 0.005\npart.FeatureManager.FeatureExtrusion2 True, False, False, 0, 0, 0.01, 0, False, False, False, False, 0, 0, False, False, False, False, True, True, True, 0, 0, False\n' select the four top edges by naming a point in space that lies on each one\npart.Extension.SelectByID2 \"\", \"EDGE\", 0, 0.02, 0.01, False, 1, Nothing, 0\npart.Extension.SelectByID2 \"\", \"EDGE\", 0, -0.02, 0.01, True, 1, Nothing, 0\npart.Extension.SelectByID2 \"\", \"EDGE\", 0.03, 0, 0.01, True, 1, Nothing, 0\npart.Extension.SelectByID2 \"\", \"EDGE\", -0.03, 0, 0.01, True, 1, Nothing, 0\n' then FeatureFillet3, with another dozen or so positional arguments\n```\n\nA traditional API automates the GUI: select first, then run the command, and you select by a name shown in the GUI (`Front Plane`) or by a point in space. The model has to work out the coordinates of a point on each edge itself. Change a dimension and forget the coordinates, and it selects something else or nothing at all, and the only error is a `False`. Fusion's API is better here: an extrude feature hands you its `endFaces` and `sideFaces`, so you can reach faces through the feature, much like FeatureScript.\n\n### In short\n\nFor an AI, a good reference meets three conditions: it lives in the code rather than in GUI state, it can be checked while the code runs, and it still holds when a dimension changes. build123d, CadQuery, KCL and FeatureScript mostly manage all three; they differ in how much typing it takes and how hard a mistake is to track down. OpenSCAD sidesteps the problem, and picking by coordinates fails all three.\n\n## Can the AI run and measure its own work?\n\nThis step opens up the widest gap. On CADGenBench, Claude Opus 5 scored 36.1% on the generation tasks with Mecado's baseline tooling and 59.6% with build123d-mcp, which can measure dimensions. ModelRift's experiment reached the same conclusion from another direction: numeric checks such as wall thickness and clearance were more reliable than renders, and the renders caught nothing that mattered.\n\n| Option | Runs locally, headless? | What a failure looks like | Can the code measure the result? |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| build123d | Yes, one Python package | A Python traceback points at the line; a kernel failure gives one terse OCCT message | Yes: volume, bounding box, faces, edges and distances are object properties |\n| CadQuery | Yes | Same, but harder to tell which step of a long chain failed | Yes |\n| OpenSCAD | Yes, one CLI program | Syntax errors come with line numbers; geometry almost never errors, a wrong part still renders | No, it can only `echo` its own variables |\n| KCL | The language parses locally; geometry needs Zoo's engine | Errors returned by the engine | Through Zoo's API |\n| FeatureScript | No, only inside Onshape | Feature errors, shown in Onshape | Yes (`evVolume`, `evBox3d` and friends), but only inside Onshape |\n| Traditional APIs | Needs a running, licensed desktop program | Usually a `False` or an empty object | Yes, with the program running |\n\nFor an AI product, the headless column sets the cost. One conversation rebuilds the part dozens of times, which adds up to thousands of builds a day. build123d, CadQuery and OpenSCAD can go in a container and run in parallel; KCL and FeatureScript call the vendor's service every time; SolidWorks will not go in a Linux container at all.\n\nThe measuring column sets the quality. OpenSCAD's booleans run on meshes and almost always produce something, so its errors are quiet: a hole 2 mm off still builds, and the model has no way to ask in code where the hole's centre is. In B-rep, a fillet that is too big fails outright. The message is not friendly (ModelRift got a bare `BRep_API: command not done`), but at least the problem surfaces.\n\nB-rep has quiet errors too. In ModelRift's threaded part, CadQuery's `union()` silently dropped the core cylinder: the volume came out at 68% of what it should have been, and the solid still reported itself as valid. What caught it was a single volume assertion.\n\nWhich measuring tools we gave our own agent, and what each one answers, is in [A CAD agent that measures its own work](/en/blog/agent-that-measures).\n\n## Geometry ceiling: mesh or B-rep\n\nThe kernel sets the limit on what parts an AI can write. OpenSCAD hands back a mesh, which is fine for 3D printing and a dead end for machining and assembly; the other five are all B-rep and export STEP.\n\n| Option | Geometry | Fillet or chamfer any edge | STEP export | Notes |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| build123d | B-rep (OCCT) | Yes | Yes | Shell, loft, sweep and thread libraries all there; OCCT's fillets and booleans are less robust than Parasolid's |\n| CadQuery | B-rep (OCCT) | Yes | Yes | Same, plus assemblies with a constraint solver |\n| OpenSCAD | Triangle mesh | No; library parameters or `hull` / `minkowski` workarounds | No | Curves are facets, resolution set by `$fn` |\n| KCL | B-rep (Zoo's own engine) | Yes | Yes | The engine is still changing quickly |\n| FeatureScript | B-rep (Parasolid) | Yes | Yes, exported through Onshape | The most mature kernel here; assemblies live in Onshape, outside FeatureScript |\n| Traditional APIs | B-rep (Parasolid, ShapeManager and others) | Yes | Yes | Sheet metal, weldments, surfacing and drawings all included |\n\nThe mesh problem goes beyond \"no STEP\". A machinist picks a cylindrical face in CAM to locate a hole and a planar face to set a datum; a mesh only has triangles. A round hole in a mesh is a ring of small flat faces, and you cannot put a fit tolerance on it. Disclosure: we built an [OpenSCAD to STEP converter](/en/openscad-to-step) that turns BOSL2 roundings back into true cylinders and tori, but threads and helical gears still come out faceted.\n\nThis is also where traditional CAD is strongest. Sheet-metal flat patterns, weldment cut lists and toleranced 2D drawings are the things manufacturing actually needs, and code CAD is weak at all of them. An AI that could drive SolidWorks or NX reliably would get the whole toolchain; what stops it is the referencing and the loop from the last two sections.\n\n## After generation: who edits the part\n\nAn AI rarely gets a part right first time. In practice a person changes a number, the AI carries on, and it goes back and forth a few times. So whether code and GUI stay in sync in both directions matters as much as raw generation ability.\n\n- **Traditional CAD**: what the AI builds through the API is a native feature tree, and an engineer can open it and edit it as if they had built it. That is the biggest advantage of the traditional APIs. The other direction is hard: once a person has edited the tree, the AI has to make sense of the features and their names again before it can continue.\n- **FeatureScript**: a generated custom feature shows up in Onshape's feature list, and the parameters declared in its `precondition` become a dialog automatically, so changing them needs no code. Anything beyond those parameters means going back to the code.\n- **KCL**: Zoo Design Studio works both ways. Point-and-click edits are written back into the KCL, and editing the code updates the view. This is where KCL's design pays off most for people and AI working together.\n- **OpenSCAD**: the Customizer turns top-level variables into sliders and drop-downs. A person can change what the AI chose to expose; everything else is code.\n- **build123d and CadQuery**: no editing GUI of their own. ocp_vscode and CQ-editor show the model but cannot write a click back into the code. That is no problem for engineers who know Python and a real barrier for people who only use a mouse.\n\nWhere the code is the single source of truth and GUI edits are written back into it, people and the AI can hand the part back and forth easily. With traditional CAD, handing the part from the AI to a person is easy and handing it back is hard. Disclosure: filling that gap for build123d is what [Forgent3D](/en/build123d-online) does. It derives a step-by-step outline and a parameter panel from the source, and numbers changed in the panel are written back into the code.\n\n## Verdicts, tool by tool\n\nPutting the five dimensions together, build123d is the most balanced choice for mechanical parts, and OpenSCAD is the least effort for small printed parts.\n\n| Option | Training data | Referring to geometry | Run and measure | Geometry ceiling | Human hand-off | Best for |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| build123d | Medium | Strong | Strong | Strong | Weak | Mechanical parts that need STEP; the back end of an AI product |\n| CadQuery | Strong | Medium; the hidden stack trips models up | Strong | Strong | Weak | Research; teams with existing CadQuery code |\n| OpenSCAD | Strong | Doesn't select; library parameters instead | Medium; runs but can't measure | Weak; mesh | Medium; Customizer | 3D-printed and small parametric parts |\n| KCL | Weak | Strong | Medium; geometry in the cloud | Medium | Strong; two-way editing | People already using Zoo |\n| FeatureScript | Weak | Strong | Medium; Onshape only | Strong | Medium; parameter dialogs | Onshape users writing reusable features |\n| Traditional APIs | Medium; mostly recorded macros | Weak | Weak | Strong | Strong to a person, weak back to the AI | Automating existing SolidWorks or Fusion workflows |\n\n### build123d: the best choice for AI-generated mechanical parts today\n\nAlmost every leading CADGenBench entry uses it. Objects are explicit, selections are Python expressions, and results can be measured and asserted in code, which is exactly what the AI's loop needs. Its weak spot is models getting method names wrong, so give the model the current API reference and a way to measure.\n\n### CadQuery: the B-rep code models know best\n\nNearly every academic dataset and benchmark uses it, so first drafts run reliably. The main trap is the hidden stack in its fluent chains. Its absence from the CADGenBench top 10 may simply mean nobody submitted with it, which is not enough to call it weaker.\n\n### OpenSCAD: easiest to get running, lowest ceiling\n\nA small syntax, a large corpus and fast renders mean even small models get it right, which is why consumer AI-for-3D-printing tools tend to pick it. The price is mesh output, no filleting of arbitrary edges and no way to measure the result; once a part has to be machined or assembled, it runs out of road.\n\n### KCL: designed for AI, held back by training data and the cloud\n\nTag-based references and two-way editing are the right ideas. But general models have barely seen it, the syntax is still changing, and its geometry has to be computed in Zoo's cloud, so it is hard to use outside Zoo's own product. We go into KCL's design choices in [A CAD IR for language models should not be a feature tree](/en/blog/cad-ir).\n\n### FeatureScript: for writing features, not parts\n\nIts query system is the most robust and its kernel, Parasolid, the strongest, but it is a language for extending Onshape with new features. Having an AI write a reusable \"thread to standard\" feature suits it well; having it model a bracket from scratch is the long way round.\n\n### Traditional CAD APIs: easy to hand to an engineer, hard to hand back to the AI\n\nSingle-session synchronous calls, no Linux containers and selection by coordinates make them a poor back end for an AI product. They fit automation inside existing workflows: batch export, property edits, families of parts from a template. A number of SolidWorks and Fusion MCP servers already do this.\n\n## If you are building AI CAD, fix the loop first\n\nThe language sets the starting point; the loop sets how far you get. In practice that means four things:\n\n1. Pick something that runs headless on your own machines and produces B-rep. Today that means build123d or CadQuery.\n2. Give the model ways to measure: volume, bounding box, hole positions and the difference from a reference part all tell it more than a render does.\n3. Put the current version's API docs in its context. Don't count on it remembering them.\n4. Let a person take over, and make sure their edits land back in the code, so the AI can pick up where they left off.\n\nOnce general models have seen enough KCL and FeatureScript, this ranking may change. The point about the loop will not.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### What is the best CAD language for AI to write?\n\nFor mechanical parts that need STEP, build123d: as of October 2026, almost every leading CADGenBench entry uses it. For small 3D-printed parts, OpenSCAD is the easiest to get right in one go.\n\n### build123d vs CadQuery: which is better for AI?\n\nBoth use the same kernel, OCCT. CadQuery has more training data, so first drafts run more often; build123d's objects and selections are more explicit, which makes it easier for the AI to check its work along the way, and the best public benchmark results come from build123d.\n\n### Can OpenSCAD export STEP?\n\nNot by itself; it only produces triangle meshes such as STL and 3MF. Getting STEP takes a converter, and curved surfaces like threads still come out faceted. The options are compared in [How to export STEP from OpenSCAD](/en/blog/openscad-to-step-guide).\n\n### Can ChatGPT or Claude write SolidWorks macros?\n\nYes, and the macros often run. But they select faces and edges by coordinates and GUI names, so a changed dimension easily breaks the selection, and you need a running, licensed copy of SolidWorks to execute and check them.\n\n### What is KCL?\n\nKCL is the modelling language Zoo designed for its own CAD. Its geometry is computed by Zoo's engine in the cloud, and Zoo Design Studio lets you edit a model both as code and through the GUI.\n\n### Can AI write Onshape FeatureScript?\n\nYes; Onshape launched a FeatureScript MCP Server in September 2026. It suits generating reusable custom features better than modelling one-off parts.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [CADGenBench leaderboard](https://huggingface.co/spaces/HuggingAI4Engineering/CADGenBench) (viewed 30 October 2026) and [repository](https://github.com/huggingface/cadgenbench)\n- [BenchLM snapshot of CADGenBench generation scores](https://benchlm.ai/md/benchmarks/cadgenbenchgeneration.md) (28 October 2026)\n- [build123d-mcp](https://pypi.org/project/build123d-mcp/)\n- [Text2CAD-Bench](https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.18430), [BenchCAD](https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.10865), [CAD-Recode](https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.14042)\n- [ModelRift: CadQuery vs OpenSCAD](https://modelrift.com/blog/cadquery-vs-openscad/), [GrandpaCAD: OpenSCAD vs CadQuery vs Build123d](https://grandpacad.com/en/blog/openscad-vs-cadquery-vs-build123d)\n- [CadQueryEval](https://github.com/danwahl/cadqueryeval)\n- [Onshape: FeatureScript MCP Server](https://www.onshape.com/en/blog/featurescript-mcp-server-use-cases-text-code-cad), [FeatureScript standard library docs](https://cad.onshape.com/FsDoc/library.html)\n- [KCL fillet docs (KCL 3.0)](https://zoo.dev/docs/kcl-std/functions/std-solid-fillet), [Zoo Design Studio releases](https://github.com/KittyCAD/modeling-app/releases)\n- Versions: [build123d](https://pypi.org/project/build123d/), [CadQuery](https://pypi.org/project/cadquery/) (PyPI, checked 30 October 2026)\n" },
  { slug: "ai-cad-languages", locale: "zh", file: "ai-cad-languages.zh.md", raw: "---\ntitle: AI 写 CAD 该用哪种代码：build123d、CadQuery、OpenSCAD、KCL、FeatureScript 与传统 CAD API 横评\ndescription: 按 AI 生成能力比较 build123d、CadQuery、OpenSCAD、KCL、FeatureScript 和 SolidWorks、Fusion 等传统 CAD API：公开评测数据、同一个零件的六种写法、闭环和几何上限。\ndate: 2026-10-30\n---\n\n先说结论：让大模型写 CAD，成败主要看三件事——模型见过多少这种代码，代码怎么指代已经建出来的面和边，AI 能不能自己把代码跑起来、量出结果。按这三条排，build123d 和 CadQuery 目前最适合 AI：Python 写、B-rep 内核、能在本地无头运行；Hugging Face 的 CADGenBench 上，截至 2026-10-30 前十名里写明工具的全是 build123d。OpenSCAD 最容易跑通，天花板也最低；KCL 的语言设计最照顾 AI，但语料少、运行要靠厂商的云；FeatureScript 和传统 CAD 的 API 本来就不是为「从零写一个零件」设计的。\n\n人用 CAD 是边点边看，选哪个面是眼睛告诉手的。模型只有文本，它写下「在顶面打个孔」的时候看不见顶面。所以 AI 建模其实是一个循环：写代码，交给内核执行，把结果渲染出来、量一量，再把图和报错喂回给模型去改。下面每一节对应这个循环里的一环。\n\n![AI 建模是一个循环，差距最大的一环是「量」](/blog/ai-cad-languages/loop-zh.png)\n\n利益相关：我在做 Forgent3D，一个基于 build123d 的 AI CAD 平台，也支持 OpenSCAD。我们为什么让 AI 写代码而不是直接生成网格，写在[《代码就是模型》](/zh/blog/code-is-model)里；文中涉及我们自己的地方会单独说明。\n\n## 六种 CAD 代码方案速览\n\n六种方案里，只有 build123d 和 CadQuery 能在自己的机器上免费、无界面地算出 B-rep（可导出 STEP 的精确几何）；OpenSCAD 只产出网格；KCL、FeatureScript 和传统 CAD API 的几何都得在厂商的程序或云端里算。\n\n| 方案 | 语言 | 几何内核与输出 | 在哪里运行 | 许可 | 版本（2026-10） |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| [build123d](https://github.com/gumyr/build123d) | Python 库 | OCCT，B-rep，导出 STEP | 本地 pip 安装，可无头运行 | Apache-2.0 | 0.13.0 |\n| [CadQuery](https://github.com/CadQuery/cadquery) | Python 库 | OCCT，B-rep，导出 STEP | 本地 pip / conda，可无头运行 | Apache-2.0 | 2.8.0 |\n| [OpenSCAD](https://openscad.org) | 自有的函数式语言 | 网格布尔（新版可切 Manifold 后端），导出 STL / 3MF，没有 STEP | 桌面程序与命令行，也有 wasm 版 | GPL | 稳定版 + 开发快照 |\n| [KCL](https://zoo.dev/docs/kcl-lang) | Zoo 的自有语言 | Zoo 自研几何引擎，B-rep，导出 STEP | Zoo Design Studio 或 API，几何在 Zoo 云端算 | 语言与客户端开源（MIT），引擎闭源 | KCL 3.0，Design Studio 1.5.3 |\n| [FeatureScript](https://cad.onshape.com/FsDoc/) | Onshape 的自有语言 | Parasolid，B-rep | 只在 Onshape 云里 | 标准库源码公开，平台商业 | 随 Onshape 更新 |\n| 传统 CAD API | SolidWorks 用 VBA / C#，Fusion 用 Python / C++，NX 用 C++ / Python 等 | 各家内核（Parasolid、ShapeManager 等），B-rep | 装好、授权好的桌面程序；SolidWorks 只有 Windows 版 | 商业 | — |\n\n这张表已经决定了一半的结果：AI 要反复「写、跑、看」，跑一次的成本、能不能在服务器上批量跑、产物是不是精确几何，都由这几列定了。\n\n## 公开评测：AI 用哪种 CAD 语言做得最好\n\n目前最硬的一份数据是 Hugging Face 的 [CADGenBench](https://huggingface.co/spaces/HuggingAI4Engineering/CADGenBench)。截至 2026-10-30，它的验证榜前十里，名字写明了建模工具的提交全部用 build123d，没有一个用 CadQuery、OpenSCAD 或 KCL。第一名是 build123d-mcp 加 Claude Opus 5.5，总分 0.830。\n\n这份卷子很接近真实工作：49 道题给一张工程图让 AI 建模，32 道题给一个 STEP 和一句修改要求（「把叶轮从 7 片改成 5 片」）。交回来的必须是 STEP，先查是不是封闭合法的实体，不合法直接零分；再按形状（占 0.4）、配合特征能不能装进同一个夹具（0.4）、孔和腔的个数（0.2）打分。OpenSCAD 只出网格，连这份卷子都交不了。\n\n但分数不全是语言给的。同一个模型、同样写 build123d，换一套工具链就能差出一截（下图）。官方基线已经会把渲染图喂回给模型；[build123d-mcp](https://pypi.org/project/build123d-mcp/) 多给的是量体积和包围盒、找孔、和输入文件做对比、导出前校验这些工具。差距主要来自「量」，而不是「看」。\n\n![同一模型接上 build123d-mcp 后，CADGenBench 生成题分数提高 4 到 24 个百分点](/blog/ai-cad-languages/chart-zh.png)\n\n读这张图要打两个折扣。三组的基线不完全相同，build123d-mcp 本身也在这几个月里不断更新；而且 agent 评测本来就抖，我们自己的评测里同一配置重跑一次能摆动 16 个点（[记录在这里](/zh/blog/eval-variance)）。+4、+7 这样的差值要谨慎看，Opus 5 的 +23.5 才算明确。图中分数取自 BenchLM 2026-10-28 的快照，前十与排行榜原页一致。\n\n其他公开结果：\n\n| 来源 | 比了哪些语言 | 测了什么 | 结果 |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| [ModelRift 博客](https://modelrift.com/blog/cadquery-vs-openscad/)（2026-09，厂商） | OpenSCAD、CadQuery | Claude Opus 5 在 Claude Code 里做 3 个打印件 | 两边都做出来了。OpenSCAD 报错 2 次、几何悄悄错 5 次；CadQuery 报错 5 次、悄悄错 4 次。CadQuery 能在代码里断言尺寸，OpenSCAD 侧只能另写 STL 解析器 |\n| [GrandpaCAD 博客](https://grandpacad.com/en/blog/openscad-vs-cadquery-vs-build123d)（厂商） | OpenSCAD、CadQuery、build123d | 同一个 gemini-3.5-flash、同一组提示 | OpenSCAD 每次生成约 0.4 个代码错误，build123d 1.4 到 1.7 个，多是方法名写错、参数顺序错 |\n| [Text2CAD-Bench](https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.18430)（2026-05，论文） | CadQuery 对比命令序列 | 600 条文字描述，7 个通用大模型 | 写成 CadQuery 代码明显更好；换成命令序列后 DeepSeek-V3.2 的无效率从 13.3% 涨到 67.3% |\n| [BenchCAD](https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.10865)（2026-05，论文） | CadQuery | 17,900 个工业零件，看图写代码 | 前沿模型能跑通但形状对不上：Claude Opus 4.7 执行率 94.0%，IoU 只有 0.274；专门训练的 2B 小模型 IoU 0.752。约 64% 的「成功」编辑悄悄改坏了别的特征 |\n| [CadQueryEval](https://github.com/danwahl/cadqueryeval)（社区，2026-09 归档） | CadQuery | 25 道文字题，和参考 STL 比 | 已有四个模型 25 题全对，题目被做满了 |\n\n表面上两类结果矛盾：厂商博客说 OpenSCAD 错得少，CADGenBench 前排却全是 build123d。其实它们量的是两件事。GrandpaCAD 数的是代码能不能跑，做的是 3D 打印小件；CADGenBench 量的是几何对不对、装不装得上。做机械零件，比的是后一件。\n\n还要提醒一句：CADGenBench 的题目是公开的（答案不公开），榜上好几行是同一位作者反复迭代工具后的提交，最高分更像上限，不是普通用法的水平。\n\n## 模型见过多少：语料与语法稳定性\n\nOpenSCAD 和 Python 系（CadQuery、build123d）在语料上占便宜，KCL 和 FeatureScript 最吃亏。但语料决定的是第一版代码能不能跑，不决定最后的零件对不对。\n\n- **OpenSCAD**：Thingiverse、Printables 和 GitHub 上十几年的存量，语法多年没大变，关键字只有几十个。模型很少编出不存在的函数。不足是语料以打印小件为主，机械零件的写法见得少。\n- **CadQuery**：沾 Python 的光，循环、函数、数学都是模型最熟的东西。学术界几乎都拿它做数据集，[CAD-Recode](https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.14042) 一篇就合成了一百万段 CadQuery 程序。\n- **build123d**：同样是 Python，但更年轻，公开代码比 CadQuery 少，API 还在 0.x 阶段。两者方法名又相似，模型容易串用，这正是 GrandpaCAD 看到的错法：方法名不存在、参数顺序错、在错的对象上调用。\n- **KCL**：出现才两三年，语法已经大改过两轮。先是位置参数全部改成关键字参数，2026 年的 KCL 3.0 又把草图换成带约束求解的 sketch block，文件头要声明 `@settings(kclVersion = 3.0)`。通用模型记住的多半是旧写法，只能现查文档。\n- **FeatureScript**：公开代码主要是 Onshape 标准库和社区自定义特征，量很小。Onshape 在 2026-09 推出的 [FeatureScript MCP Server](https://www.onshape.com/en/blog/featurescript-mcp-server-use-cases-text-code-cad) 走的是生成、运行、调试、再改的循环，而不是指望模型一次写对。\n- **传统 API**：SolidWorks 的 VBA 宏在论坛上数量很多，但大多是录宏录出来的：`SelectByID2` 加坐标、`Sketch1`、`Boss-Extrude1`。模型从中学到的是怎么复现某人当时的点击，不是怎么表达设计。Fusion 的 Python API 规整一些，样例也齐。\n\nAPI 不稳定的代价，在 AI 场景里会被放大：人升级后看一眼变更日志就行，模型却要等下一轮训练。靠提示词或工具把当前版本的文档递给模型，是每个语言都绕不开的活。\n\n## 怎么指代已有的面和边：同一个零件的六种写法\n\n同一个零件：60×40×10 的板，顶面四条边倒 R2，中心打 Ø10 通孔。六种写法最大的差别，在怎么说出「顶面那四条边」。这是 AI 写 CAD 最容易错的地方，因为模型看不到它要选的东西。\n\n### build123d\n\n```python\nfrom build123d import *\n\nplate = Box(60, 40, 10)\ntop_edges = plate.edges().group_by(Axis.Z)[-1]   # 按 Z 分组，最高一组 = 顶面四条边\nplate = fillet(top_edges, radius=2)\nplate -= Cylinder(radius=5, height=10)\n```\n\n选择就是一句普通的 Python 表达式：取全部边，按 Z 分组，取最高的一组。中间结果是变量，AI 可以先 `print(len(top_edges))` 确认选中 4 条再往下走。这段代码我们在 build123d 0.13.0 上跑过，体积 23,046 mm³。\n\n### CadQuery\n\n```python\nimport cadquery as cq\n\nplate = (\n    cq.Workplane(\"XY\").box(60, 40, 10)\n    .faces(\">Z\").edges().fillet(2)\n    .faces(\">Z\").workplane().hole(10)\n)\n```\n\n`\">Z\"` 这种字符串选择器很短，模型也见得多。代价是整条链共享一个隐式的栈：`.workplane()` 的原点落在哪、`.hole()` 打在哪，取决于上一步留下了什么。链一长，模型就容易搞错当前栈上是什么。\n\n### OpenSCAD\n\n```openscad\ninclude <BOSL2/std.scad>\n$fn = 64;\n\ndifference() {\n    cuboid([60, 40, 10], rounding = 2, edges = TOP);  // 顶面四条边倒圆\n    cyl(d = 10, h = 12);                              // 中心通孔\n}\n```\n\nOpenSCAD 根本不选边。倒圆要么靠 BOSL2 这类库做成参数（`edges = TOP`），要么自己用 `hull`、`minkowski` 拼。好处是不存在选错边；坏处是库没覆盖的情况，比如给布尔之后才出现的交线倒圆，基本做不了。这段在 OpenSCAD（Manifold 后端）上渲染出 474 个三角面，体积和 build123d 版几乎一样，但圆角是折线拼的。\n\n### KCL\n\n```kcl\n@settings(defaultLengthUnit = mm, kclVersion = 3.0)\n\nprofile = sketch(on = XY) {\n  front = line(start = [var -30mm, var -20mm], end = [var 30mm, var -20mm])\n  right = line(start = [var 30mm, var -20mm], end = [var 30mm, var 20mm])\n  back = line(start = [var 30mm, var 20mm], end = [var -30mm, var 20mm])\n  left = line(start = [var -30mm, var 20mm], end = [var -30mm, var -20mm])\n}\nplateRegion = region(segments = [profile.left, profile.front])\nplate = extrude(plateRegion, length = 10mm, tagEnd = $top)\n\nrounded = fillet(\n  plate,\n  edges = [\n    { sideFaces = [plateRegion.tags.front, plate.faces.top] },\n    { sideFaces = [plateRegion.tags.right, plate.faces.top] },\n    { sideFaces = [plateRegion.tags.back, plate.faces.top] },\n    { sideFaces = [plateRegion.tags.left, plate.faces.top] }\n  ],\n  radius = 2mm,\n)\n// 中心孔用 hole::hole(...) 或 subtract(...)，从略\n```\n\nKCL 用标签指代：给草图里的线起名，拉伸时给顶面起名，再用「哪两个面夹着的边」说出一条边。名字是写代码的人自己起的，不依赖几何计算，对模型很友好。代价是啰嗦：四条边写四遍，而且建草图时就得想好以后要引用什么。写法按 Zoo 当前文档的 KCL 3.0 示例改写，没有实际运行。\n\n### FeatureScript\n\n```featurescript\nannotation { \"Feature Type Name\" : \"Plate\" }\nexport const plate = defineFeature(function(context is Context, id is Id, definition is map)\n    precondition {}\n    {\n        fCuboid(context, id + \"box\", {\n                \"corner1\" : vector(-30, -20, 0) * millimeter,\n                \"corner2\" : vector(30, 20, 10) * millimeter\n        });\n        // 顶面 = box 生成的面里，落在 z = 10 平面上的那个\n        const top = qCoincidesWithPlane(qCreatedBy(id + \"box\", EntityType.FACE),\n                plane(vector(0, 0, 10) * millimeter, vector(0, 0, 1)));\n        opFillet(context, id + \"fillet\", { \"entities\" : top, \"radius\" : 2 * millimeter });\n        fCylinder(context, id + \"pin\", {\n                \"bottomCenter\" : vector(0, 0, 0) * millimeter,\n                \"topCenter\" : vector(0, 0, 10) * millimeter,\n                \"radius\" : 5 * millimeter\n        });\n        opBoolean(context, id + \"cut\", {\n                \"tools\" : qCreatedBy(id + \"pin\", EntityType.BODY),\n                \"targets\" : qCreatedBy(id + \"box\", EntityType.BODY),\n                \"operationType\" : BooleanOperationType.SUBTRACTION\n        });\n    }, {});\n```\n\nFeatureScript 用查询（query）指代：「box 这一步生成的、落在 z = 10 平面上的面」。查询说的是来历加条件，上游参数改了也能找回同一个面，这是 Onshape 参数化稳定的原因。但它是为写「特征」设计的：先定义一个 feature，再到 Part Studio 里用，id、单位、context 这些仪式让代码长了一倍。文件头和 import 从略，函数签名已对照 FsDoc 核对。\n\n### 传统 CAD API（以 SolidWorks VBA 为例）\n\n```vb\n' SolidWorks VBA 宏（节选）。API 的长度单位是米\nSet part = swApp.NewPart\npart.Extension.SelectByID2 \"Front Plane\", \"PLANE\", 0, 0, 0, False, 0, Nothing, 0\npart.SketchManager.InsertSketch True\npart.SketchManager.CreateCenterRectangle 0, 0, 0, 0.03, 0.02, 0\npart.SketchManager.CreateCircleByRadius 0, 0, 0, 0.005\npart.FeatureManager.FeatureExtrusion2 True, False, False, 0, 0, 0.01, 0, False, False, False, False, 0, 0, False, False, False, False, True, True, True, 0, 0, False\n' 选顶面四条边：报一个落在边上的空间坐标\npart.Extension.SelectByID2 \"\", \"EDGE\", 0, 0.02, 0.01, False, 1, Nothing, 0\npart.Extension.SelectByID2 \"\", \"EDGE\", 0, -0.02, 0.01, True, 1, Nothing, 0\npart.Extension.SelectByID2 \"\", \"EDGE\", 0.03, 0, 0.01, True, 1, Nothing, 0\npart.Extension.SelectByID2 \"\", \"EDGE\", -0.03, 0, 0.01, True, 1, Nothing, 0\n' 然后调用 FeatureFillet3，又是十几个位置参数\n```\n\n传统 API 是界面操作的自动化：先选中再执行，选的方式是界面里的名字（`Front Plane`）或一个空间坐标。模型要自己算出边上某一点的坐标；尺寸一改坐标没跟着改，就会选到别的东西，或什么都没选中，而报错只是一个 `False`。Fusion 的 API 好一些：拉伸特征直接给出 `endFaces`、`sideFaces`，可以顺着特征拿面，思路接近 FeatureScript。\n\n### 小结\n\n对 AI 来说，好的指代要满足三条：写在代码里，不靠界面状态；运行时能检查选中了什么；尺寸改了还成立。build123d、CadQuery、KCL、FeatureScript 都基本做到，差别在写起来多费劲、出错时多难查。OpenSCAD 绕开了这个问题，坐标点选三条都不占。\n\n## 能不能自己跑、自己量：AI 的反馈闭环\n\n这一环拉开的差距最大。CADGenBench 上，Claude Opus 5 用 Mecado 的基线工具链时生成题得 36.1%，接上能量尺寸的 build123d-mcp 后得 59.6%。ModelRift 的实验也得出一样的结论：量壁厚、量间隙这类数值检查比看渲染图可靠，渲染图没抓到任何要紧的错。\n\n| 方案 | 能否本地无头运行 | 出错时长什么样 | 能否在代码里量结果 |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| build123d | 能，装一个 Python 包 | Python 堆栈指到行；内核失败时只有 OCCT 的一句话 | 能，体积、包围盒、面、边、距离都是对象属性 |\n| CadQuery | 能 | 同上，长链里更难定位是哪一步 | 能 |\n| OpenSCAD | 能，一个命令行程序 | 语法错有行号；几何几乎不报错，错了也照样出网格 | 不能，只能 `echo` 自己的变量 |\n| KCL | 语言能本地解析，几何要连 Zoo 的引擎 | 引擎返回的错误 | 要走 Zoo 的接口 |\n| FeatureScript | 不能，只在 Onshape 里跑 | 特征报错，在 Onshape 里看 | 能（`evVolume`、`evBox3d` 等），但只在 Onshape 里 |\n| 传统 API | 要开着一份授权的桌面程序 | 多半是返回 `False` 或空对象 | 能，同样要开着程序 |\n\n做 AI 产品时，「无头」这一列决定成本。一个对话要重建几十次，一天就是成千上万次：build123d、CadQuery、OpenSCAD 可以扔进容器并发跑；KCL 和 FeatureScript 每次都要请求厂商的服务；SolidWorks 连 Linux 容器都进不去。\n\n「量」这一列决定质量。OpenSCAD 的布尔运算在网格上做，几乎总能出结果，所以错误是「安静」的：孔偏了 2 mm，代码照样跑通，模型也没法在代码里问「这个孔的中心在哪」。B-rep 的倒角过大会直接失败，报错虽然不好读（ModelRift 遇到的是一句 `BRep_API: command not done`），至少把问题暴露出来了。\n\nB-rep 也有安静的错：ModelRift 的螺纹件里，CadQuery 的 `union()` 悄悄丢掉了芯部圆柱，体积只有应有的 68%，形体却仍然「合法」。能抓到它的，恰恰是一句体积断言。\n\n我们给自己的 agent 做了哪些「量」的工具，各自回答什么问题，写在[《一个会自己量尺寸的 CAD agent》](/zh/blog/agent-that-measures)里。\n\n## 几何能力的上限：网格还是 B-rep\n\nAI 能写出什么零件，上限由内核定。OpenSCAD 交的是网格，做 3D 打印够用，进不了机加工和装配流程；其余五种都是 B-rep，能导出 STEP。\n\n| 方案 | 几何表示 | 任意边倒圆 / 倒角 | 导出 STEP | 备注 |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| build123d | B-rep（OCCT） | 能 | 能 | 抽壳、放样、扫掠、螺纹库都有；OCCT 的倒圆和布尔不如 Parasolid 稳 |\n| CadQuery | B-rep（OCCT） | 能 | 能 | 同上，另有带约束求解的装配 |\n| OpenSCAD | 三角网格 | 不能，靠库参数或 `hull` / `minkowski` 拼 | 不能 | 曲面都是折面，精度由 `$fn` 定 |\n| KCL | B-rep（Zoo 自研引擎） | 能 | 能 | 引擎还在快速迭代 |\n| FeatureScript | B-rep（Parasolid） | 能 | 能，经 Onshape 导出 | 内核最成熟；装配在 Onshape 里做，不归 FeatureScript 管 |\n| 传统 API | B-rep（Parasolid、ShapeManager 等） | 能 | 能 | 钣金、焊件、曲面、工程图一应俱全 |\n\n网格的限制不只是「导不出 STEP」。做机加工的人要在 CAM 里选圆柱面定孔、选平面定基准，网格里只有三角形；圆孔在网格里是一圈小平面，配合公差也没法标。利益相关：我们做了一个[把 OpenSCAD 转成 STEP 的工具](/zh/openscad-to-step)，BOSL2 的倒圆能还原成真正的圆柱面和圆环面，但螺纹、斜齿轮这类只能仍是折面。\n\n传统 CAD 的优势也在这里：钣金展开、焊件清单、带公差的二维工程图，这些制造环节真正要的东西，代码 CAD 目前都弱。AI 如果能稳定地驱动 SolidWorks 或 NX，拿到的是整套工具链；问题是前两节说的指代和闭环。\n\n## 生成之后谁来改：人怎么接手\n\nAI 很少一次做对，实际用法是人改一个数、AI 接着改，来回几轮。所以代码和界面能不能双向同步，和生成能力本身一样影响能不能用。\n\n- **传统 CAD**：AI 通过 API 建出来的是原生特征树，工程师打开就能像自己建的一样改。这是传统 API 最大的优势。反过来就难了：人改过之后，AI 要重新读懂特征树和里面的名字，才能接着改。\n- **FeatureScript**：生成的自定义特征出现在 Onshape 的特征列表里，`precondition` 里声明的参数会自动变成对话框，改参数不用碰代码。参数之外的改动，还是得回到代码。\n- **KCL**：Zoo Design Studio 是双向的，在界面里点选做的修改会写回 KCL，改代码界面也跟着变。这是 KCL 在设计上最照顾人机协作的地方。\n- **OpenSCAD**：Customizer 把顶层变量变成滑块和下拉框，人能改的只有 AI 预留的参数，剩下的都是代码。\n- **build123d / CadQuery**：没有自带的编辑界面，ocp_vscode、CQ-editor 只能看不能点选写回。对会 Python 的工程师不成问题，对只用鼠标的人是门槛。\n\n代码是唯一真源、界面修改写回代码的方案，人和 AI 来回交接最顺；传统 CAD 是 AI 交给人容易，人再交回给 AI 难。利益相关：补 build123d 这块短板正是 [Forgent3D](/zh/build123d-online) 在做的事，从源码推出一份逐步的说明书和参数面板，在界面里改的数会写回代码。\n\n## 逐个结论\n\n五个维度放在一起，做机械零件时 build123d 最均衡，做打印小件时 OpenSCAD 最省事。\n\n| 方案 | 语料 | 指代几何 | 自己跑、自己量 | 几何上限 | 人接手 | 适合 |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| build123d | 中 | 强 | 强 | 强 | 弱 | 要 STEP 的机械零件；AI 产品的后端 |\n| CadQuery | 强 | 中，隐式栈容易乱 | 强 | 强 | 弱 | 研究；已有 CadQuery 代码的团队 |\n| OpenSCAD | 强 | 不选，靠库参数 | 中，能跑不能量 | 弱，网格 | 中，Customizer | 3D 打印件、参数化小件 |\n| KCL | 弱 | 强 | 中，几何在云端 | 中 | 强，双向编辑 | 已在用 Zoo 的人 |\n| FeatureScript | 弱 | 强 | 中，只在 Onshape | 强 | 中，参数对话框 | Onshape 用户写可复用的特征 |\n| 传统 API | 中，多为录宏 | 弱 | 弱 | 强 | 交给人强，交回 AI 弱 | 在既有 SolidWorks / Fusion 流程里做自动化 |\n\n### build123d：目前 AI 生成机械零件的最好选择\n\nCADGenBench 前排几乎都是它。对象是显式的，选择是 Python 表达式，结果能在代码里量、能断言，正好对上 AI 的闭环。短板是模型常写错方法名，所以要把当前版本的 API 参考和量尺寸的工具一起交给模型。\n\n### CadQuery：模型最熟的 B-rep 代码\n\n学术数据集和基准几乎都用它，第一版代码的成功率有保障。主要的坑是链式写法的隐式栈。CADGenBench 前十里没有它，可能只是没人拿它提交，不足以说明它更弱。\n\n### OpenSCAD：最容易跑通，天花板最低\n\n语法小、语料多、渲染快，小模型也能写对，所以做消费级 AI 打印工具的厂商都偏爱它。代价是网格输出、没有任意边倒圆、结果量不了，零件一要进制造或装配就到头了。\n\n### KCL：为 AI 设计的语言，卡在语料和云上\n\n标签式指代和双向编辑都是对的方向。但通用模型几乎没见过它，语法还在变，几何又必须在 Zoo 的云里算，在 Zoo 自己的产品之外很难用起来。KCL 的设计取舍，我们在[《给语言模型用的 CAD IR》](/zh/blog/cad-ir)里谈得更细。\n\n### FeatureScript：写特征，不写零件\n\n它的查询系统最稳，Parasolid 内核最强，但它是给 Onshape 扩展新特征的语言。让 AI 写一个「按标准生成螺纹」的可复用特征很合适，让它从零做一个支架就绕远了。\n\n### 传统 CAD API：能交给工程师，难交回 AI\n\n单会话同步调用、Linux 容器进不去、靠坐标点选，让它做 AI 产品的后端很吃力。合适的用法是在已有流程里做自动化：批量导出、改属性、按模板生成系列件，市面上已有不少 SolidWorks 和 Fusion 的 MCP 服务在做这件事。\n\n## 结语：做 AI CAD，先修闭环\n\n语言决定起点，闭环决定终点。落到做法上是四条：\n\n1. 选能在本地无头运行、产出 B-rep 的方案，今天就是 build123d 或 CadQuery。\n2. 把「量」交给模型：体积、包围盒、孔的位置、和参考件的差异，都比渲染图有用。\n3. 把当前版本的 API 文档放进上下文，别指望模型记得。\n4. 让人能接手，并且人的修改能回到代码里，AI 才能接着改。\n\n等通用模型见过足够多的 KCL 和 FeatureScript，这个排序可能会变；闭环这一条不会。\n\n## 常见问题\n\n### AI 生成 CAD 用哪种语言最好？\n\n要 STEP、要做机械零件，用 build123d：截至 2026-10，CADGenBench 前排的提交几乎都用它。只做 3D 打印小件，OpenSCAD 最容易一次跑通。\n\n### build123d 和 CadQuery 哪个更适合 AI？\n\n两者用同一个内核（OCCT）。CadQuery 语料更多，第一版代码更容易跑；build123d 的对象和选择更显式，方便 AI 在中途检查，公开基准上的最好成绩也来自 build123d。\n\n### OpenSCAD 能导出 STEP 吗？\n\n本身不能，它只产出三角网格（STL、3MF 等）。要 STEP 只能借助转换工具，螺纹这类曲面转出来仍是折面；几种办法的对比见[《OpenSCAD 怎么导出 STEP》](/zh/blog/openscad-to-step-guide)。\n\n### ChatGPT、Claude 能直接写 SolidWorks 宏吗？\n\n能写，也常常能跑，但选面选边靠坐标和界面名字，尺寸一改就容易选错；而且得开着一份授权的 SolidWorks 才能执行和验证。\n\n### KCL 是什么？\n\nZoo 公司为自家 CAD 设计的建模语言。几何在 Zoo 云端的引擎里计算，可以在 Zoo Design Studio 里和界面双向编辑。\n\n### FeatureScript 能用来让 AI 建模吗？\n\n能，Onshape 在 2026-09 推出了 FeatureScript MCP Server。但它更适合生成可复用的自定义特征，而不是一次性的零件。\n\n## 来源\n\n- [CADGenBench 排行榜](https://huggingface.co/spaces/HuggingAI4Engineering/CADGenBench)（2026-10-30 查看）与[代码仓库](https://github.com/huggingface/cadgenbench)\n- [BenchLM 的 CADGenBench 生成题快照](https://benchlm.ai/md/benchmarks/cadgenbenchgeneration.md)（2026-10-28）\n- [build123d-mcp](https://pypi.org/project/build123d-mcp/)\n- [Text2CAD-Bench](https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.18430)、[BenchCAD](https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.10865)、[CAD-Recode](https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.14042)\n- [ModelRift：CadQuery vs OpenSCAD](https://modelrift.com/blog/cadquery-vs-openscad/)、[GrandpaCAD：OpenSCAD vs CadQuery vs Build123d](https://grandpacad.com/en/blog/openscad-vs-cadquery-vs-build123d)\n- [CadQueryEval](https://github.com/danwahl/cadqueryeval)\n- [Onshape：FeatureScript MCP Server](https://www.onshape.com/en/blog/featurescript-mcp-server-use-cases-text-code-cad)、[FeatureScript 标准库文档](https://cad.onshape.com/FsDoc/library.html)\n- [KCL fillet 文档（KCL 3.0）](https://zoo.dev/docs/kcl-std/functions/std-solid-fillet)、[Zoo Design Studio 发布记录](https://github.com/KittyCAD/modeling-app/releases)\n- 版本号：[build123d](https://pypi.org/project/build123d/)、[CadQuery](https://pypi.org/project/cadquery/)（PyPI，2026-10-30 查看）\n" },
  { slug: "brep-not-for-transformers", locale: "en", file: "brep-not-for-transformers.en.md", raw: "---\ntitle: B-rep is not for transformers: why our AI does not write it directly\ndescription: A B-rep is exact and every CAD program reads it, so why not have the model generate one? We measured what it is made of on our own parts: pointers that must all land, numbers that must agree in several places at once, many spellings of the same solid, and no record of what anything is for.\ndate: 2026-10-19\n---\n\nAfter [the article on why our AI writes CAD code instead of a mesh](/en/blog/code-is-model), the next question is always the same. Fine, a mesh does not know what it is. A B-rep does: it is exact, it is what a STEP file holds, and Fusion, SolidWorks and every CAM program read it. So why have the model write code at all? Why not generate the B-rep directly?\n\nOur short answer: B-rep is the right thing to deliver and the wrong thing for a transformer to write. A language model does not produce a PDF byte by byte; it writes the text, and a program that never makes layout mistakes produces the PDF. For a part, that program is the CAD kernel, and what it produces is the B-rep.\n\nThe long answer is below, measured on our own parts.\n\n## What a B-rep is made of\n\nTake about the smallest part with a feature: a 40 × 30 × 10 mm plate with a Ø6 hole through the middle. As code it is three lines:\n\n```python\nplate = Box(40, 30, 10, align=(Align.MIN, Align.MIN, Align.MIN))\nhole = Pos(20, 15, 5) * Cylinder(3, 10)\nmodel = Model(bodies=[Body(\"plate\", geometry=plate - hole)])\n```\n\nWith the imports and a one-line description, the file is 148 tokens. The STEP file our exporter writes for the same plate is 504 lines and 6,835 tokens: 434 entities, 500 references from one entity to another, 397 numbers.\n\nThose entities describe two things tied together. Topology: one solid, 7 faces, 15 edges, 10 vertices, which loop of edges bounds which face, which two faces share each edge. Geometry: the plane or cylinder each face lies on, the line or circle each edge follows, the point where each vertex sits. Across our parts, about half of a STEP file is a third thing: every edge written out again in 2D, in the coordinates of each face it bounds.\n\nHere is the wall of the hole, and one of its rim edges, as they appear in the file:\n\n```\n#402 = ADVANCED_FACE('',(#403),#282,.F.);\n#403 = FACE_BOUND('',#404,.F.);\n#404 = EDGE_LOOP('',(#405,#406,#427,#428));\n#282 = CYLINDRICAL_SURFACE('',#283,3.);\n#283 = AXIS2_PLACEMENT_3D('',#284,#285,#286);\n#284 = CARTESIAN_POINT('',(20.,15.,0.));\n#285 = DIRECTION('',(0.,0.,1.));\n#286 = DIRECTION('',(1.,0.,0.));\n#265 = EDGE_CURVE('',#266,#266,#268,.T.);\n#266 = VERTEX_POINT('',#267);\n#267 = CARTESIAN_POINT('',(23.,15.,10.));\n#268 = SURFACE_CURVE('',#269,(#274,#281),.PCURVE_S1.);\n#269 = CIRCLE('',#270,3.);\n#270 = AXIS2_PLACEMENT_3D('',#271,#272,#273);\n#271 = CARTESIAN_POINT('',(20.,15.,10.));\n```\n\nFour things about this make it a poor thing for a transformer to write.\n\n## It is a graph, written as pointers\n\nA transformer writes one token after another. A B-rep is a graph with cycles: a face points at its loops, a loop at its edges, every edge is shared by two faces, every vertex by each edge that ends there. To write it as a sequence you number every entity and write the numbers, `#403`, `#404`, `#266`. The plate needs 500 of them, and each has to land on exactly the right entity, often hundreds of tokens back. A pointer that is off by one does not give a slightly wrong plate. It gives something that is not a solid.\n\nCode has references too, but they are names: `plate`, `hole`. A name that does not exist stops the build on the line that used it.\n\n## One number lives in several places, and they all have to agree\n\nIn the code, the hole's radius is written once. In the STEP it is written five times: the two rim circles, their two 2D copies on the top and bottom faces, and the cylinder. It is implied three more times, in the two vertices and the seam at x = 23, which are only right if they lie on those circles. The hole's position appears in eight points.\n\nChange the hole from Ø6 to Ø6.4. In the code that is one number. In the STEP, exactly eight values in eight entities change, and they have to change together. Leave one vertex at x = 23 while its circle says 3.2, and the vertex no longer lies on its own edge; the tolerance written into the file is 1E-07 mm.\n\nA transformer samples numbers one at a time, and a sample is approximately right. A B-rep needs numbers that sit far apart in the sequence to agree to a ten-thousandth of a micron. In code that agreement is not the model's job: where the hole meets the top face is an intersection the kernel works out, so it is right by construction.\n\n## Change how many of something there are and it is a new file\n\nEdits to dimensions stay local as long as the topology holds. On our box generator, making the inside 5 mm longer is one token in the code and 753 entities in the STEP, 11% of the file. Change a count and it is a different file: a spur gear going from 20 teeth to 21 is one token in the code and rewrites three quarters of the STEP's entities, and from #16288 on everything is renumbered.\n\nMost of what people ask an assistant for is the second kind of edit: one more tooth, two more holes, a third compartment. On a B-rep every one of those is a regeneration.\n\n## The same solid has many spellings\n\nWe built the plate twice: once as a box minus a cylinder, once as a sketch of a rectangle with a circle taken out, extruded. Same 7 faces, same volume to the hundredth of a cubic millimetre. 61% of the lines in the two STEP files differ. The cylinder is the seventh face in one and the fifth in the other, the planes have different origins, the hole's seam is parameterised the other way round, and one loop has its orientation flag flipped.\n\nTo be fair to the exporter, it is consistent where it can be: cutting two holes in the opposite order gave a byte-identical file. But \"this plate\" is still not one sequence. It is a family of equally valid ones, and a model trained to reproduce one of them is penalised for writing another.\n\n## And it does not know what anything is for\n\nNothing in those 434 entities says \"hole\", let alone \"Ø6 because an M6 screw goes through\", \"these four holes are a pattern\" or \"this wall is 2 mm because it gets printed\". Every face, edge and point in the file has an empty name. The intent lives in the code, in names, constants and comments. The B-rep is what is left after the code has run.\n\nSize, the argument people usually reach for first, matters less than any of the above. Across our 28 generators the STEP has a median of 22 times as many tokens as the model.py. For a simple turned or prismatic part it is only 3 to 9 times, and OCCT's own B-rep format for our bolt-circle flange is about as long as its code. The ratio explodes where geometry repeats: a gear or a Gridfinity baseplate writes every tooth and every pocket out in full, which the code does with one loop.\n\n## What others found\n\nResearch has tried both roads. DeepCAD, one of the first generative models of CAD (2021), chose command sequences over B-rep on purpose: \"a command sequence can be easily converted into a B-rep, but the converse is hard, as different command sequences may result in the same B-rep.\"\n\nThe models that do generate B-rep treat validity as their headline number:\n\n- SolidGen, a transformer that writes vertices, edges and faces through pointers, handles only lines, arcs and simple surfaces. It trains on parts with 8 to 130 faces and reports 83–88% valid solids.\n- BrepGen, a diffusion model, recovers the topology by merging duplicated nodes and sewing the faces together in OpenCascade. It reports 62.9% and says plainly that it \"does not guarantee watertight solids\".\n- Newer models do well on small parts and fall off as parts grow. One reports 99% valid under ten faces and 74.5% at 21 to 30.\n- A 2026 paper that has a language model write STEP text directly trains only on files under 500 entities (our plate has 434), because \"small errors in entity ordering or identifier usage can render the entire file invalid.\"\n\nThe clearest case is a company. When Zoo launched Text-to-CAD in December 2023, it generated B-rep surfaces you downloaded as STEP. In their own account, written in February 2026: \"we initially pursued direct Text-to-B-Rep generation methods… Two developments ultimately inspired us to change course\": their own CAD language, KCL, and the rise of large language models. Their agent now returns only KCL code, and the API endpoints that produced STEP directly were removed in August 2026. Writing about models that generate meshes, they put the whole argument in one line: \"It is like training on compiled output instead of source code.\"\n\nNot everyone agrees. Mike Haley of Autodesk says their neural CAD models \"directly reason in geometry, rather than speaking to an API about geometry\", and puts the case against code bluntly: \"Tasking an LLM to work with a parametric CAD API is like hiring a plumber who has read every book on plumbing but has never touched a pipe.\" We agree with the diagnosis, not the cure. A model that writes code and never looks at the result is that plumber. Ours builds after every edit and reads back what the kernel made of it.\n\n## Where B-rep belongs\n\nNone of this is an argument against B-rep. It is what we deliver: every STEP you download from Forgent3D is a B-rep the kernel built. It is also what the agent checks its work against. After every build it reads a report computed from the B-rep, every cylinder's radius and where its axis runs, the position of every flat face ([how that works](/en/blog/agent-that-measures)). The model reads a summary of the B-rep, never the B-rep itself.\n\nB-rep is also a good input. Neural networks that read a B-rep as a graph of faces and edges, to classify a part or label its faces, work well. Being read by a graph network is a different job from being written by a sequence model.\n\n## So what should the model write?\n\nCode that a kernel compiles: short enough to read, with names instead of pointers, each number written once, and the kernel making sure the geometry agrees with itself. Which code, and what it has to carry beyond geometry, is a question of its own, and the subject of [the next article](/en/blog/cad-ir).\n" },
  { slug: "brep-not-for-transformers", locale: "zh", file: "brep-not-for-transformers.zh.md", raw: "---\ntitle: B-rep 不适合 Transformer：为什么我们不让 AI 直接写 B-rep\ndescription: B-rep 精确，所有 CAD 软件都认，为什么不让模型直接生成它？我们在自己的零件上量了它由什么组成：一个都不能指错的指针，要在好几处同时对上的数，同一个实体的好几种写法，以及没有一处记着设计意图。\ndate: 2026-10-19\n---\n\n写完[为什么我们让 AI 写 CAD 代码而不是生成网格](/zh/blog/code-is-model)之后，最常被问到的是下一个问题。网格不知道自己是什么，这没错；可 B-rep 知道。它精确，STEP 文件里存的就是它，Fusion、SolidWorks 和所有 CAM 软件都读它。那为什么还要让模型写代码？为什么不直接生成 B-rep？\n\n短的回答是：B-rep 是该交付的东西，不是该让 Transformer 写的东西。语言模型不会一个字节一个字节地吐出一份 PDF，它写文字，排版交给一个从不排错的程序。零件也一样，那个程序是 CAD 内核，它的产物就是 B-rep。\n\n长的回答在下面，数字都是在我们自己的零件上量的。\n\n## B-rep 由什么组成\n\n拿一个最小的、带特征的零件：一块 40 × 30 × 10 mm 的板，正中一个 Ø6 通孔。写成代码是三行：\n\n```python\nplate = Box(40, 30, 10, align=(Align.MIN, Align.MIN, Align.MIN))\nhole = Pos(20, 15, 5) * Cylinder(3, 10)\nmodel = Model(bodies=[Body(\"plate\", geometry=plate - hole)])\n```\n\n连同 import 和一行说明，整个文件 148 个 token。我们的导出器给同一块板写出的 STEP 是 504 行、6835 个 token：434 个实体，实体之间 500 处引用，397 个数。\n\n这些实体描述的是两样绑在一起的东西。拓扑：1 个实体、7 个面、15 条边、10 个顶点，哪一圈边围着哪个面，每条边由哪两个面共享。几何：每个面落在哪个平面或圆柱面上，每条边沿着哪条直线或圆，每个顶点在哪个点。在我们的零件里，STEP 文件大约一半是第三样东西：每条边在它所围的每个面的坐标系里，再用 2D 写一遍。\n\n下面是孔壁那个面和它的一条边沿，原样摘自文件：\n\n```\n#402 = ADVANCED_FACE('',(#403),#282,.F.);\n#403 = FACE_BOUND('',#404,.F.);\n#404 = EDGE_LOOP('',(#405,#406,#427,#428));\n#282 = CYLINDRICAL_SURFACE('',#283,3.);\n#283 = AXIS2_PLACEMENT_3D('',#284,#285,#286);\n#284 = CARTESIAN_POINT('',(20.,15.,0.));\n#285 = DIRECTION('',(0.,0.,1.));\n#286 = DIRECTION('',(1.,0.,0.));\n#265 = EDGE_CURVE('',#266,#266,#268,.T.);\n#266 = VERTEX_POINT('',#267);\n#267 = CARTESIAN_POINT('',(23.,15.,10.));\n#268 = SURFACE_CURVE('',#269,(#274,#281),.PCURVE_S1.);\n#269 = CIRCLE('',#270,3.);\n#270 = AXIS2_PLACEMENT_3D('',#271,#272,#273);\n#271 = CARTESIAN_POINT('',(20.,15.,10.));\n```\n\n它有四个地方，不适合让 Transformer 来写。\n\n## 它是一张图，写成了指针\n\nTransformer 一个 token 接一个 token 往下写。B-rep 是一张带环的图：面指向它的边圈，边圈指向它的边，每条边被两个面共享，每个顶点被连到它的每条边共享。要把它写成序列，只能给每个实体编号，再写编号：`#403`、`#404`、`#266`。这块板要写 500 个这样的编号，每一个都得落在恰好那个实体上，常常隔着几百个 token。指错一个，得到的不是一块稍微不对的板，而是一个根本不成实体的东西。\n\n代码里也有引用，但引用的是名字：`plate`、`hole`。名字不存在，构建就停在用到它的那一行。\n\n## 同一个数住在好几处，必须处处对上\n\n代码里，孔的半径写一次。STEP 里写了五次：上下两圈边沿的圆、这两个圆在顶面和底面上的 2D 副本、圆柱面本身。另外还隐含了三次：两个顶点和 x = 23 处那条接缝，它们只有落在那些圆上才对。孔的位置出现在八个点里。\n\n把孔从 Ø6 改成 Ø6.4。代码里是一个数。STEP 里恰好有八个实体里的八个值要变，而且必须一起变。漏掉一个顶点，让它还停在 x = 23，而它的圆已经是 3.2，这个顶点就不在自己的边上了。文件里写着的公差是 1E-07 mm。\n\nTransformer 一个一个地采样数字，采出来的数大致是对的。B-rep 要求序列里相隔很远的数，彼此对上到万分之一微米。写代码时，这件事不归模型管：孔和顶面在哪里相交，是内核算出来的交线，天生就对。\n\n## 数量一变，就是另一份文件\n\n只要拓扑不变，改尺寸只动局部。在我们的盒子生成器上，把内腔加长 5 mm，代码里改一个 token，STEP 里变 753 个实体，占文件的 11%。改数量就是另一份文件了：直齿轮从 20 齿改成 21 齿，代码里还是一个 token，STEP 里四分之三的实体都变了，从 #16288 起全部重新编号。\n\n人们找助手改模型，多半是第二种：多一个齿、多两个孔、再加一格。在 B-rep 上，每一次都是重新生成。\n\n## 同一个实体有很多种写法\n\n我们把这块板建了两次：一次是长方体减圆柱，一次是在草图里画一个矩形、挖掉一个圆，再拉伸。同样 7 个面，体积对到百分之一立方毫米。两份 STEP 有 61% 的行不一样：圆柱面在一份里是第七个面，在另一份里是第五个；几个平面的原点不同；孔的接缝参数方向相反；还有一个边圈的方向标记是反的。\n\n说句公道话，导出器在能一致的地方是一致的：两个孔换个顺序挖，导出来的文件逐字节相同。但「这块板」仍然不是一个序列，而是一族同样正确的序列。训练模型去复现其中一种，它写出另一种同样对的，也会被扣分。\n\n## 而且它不知道每样东西是干什么的\n\n这 434 个实体里，没有一处写着「孔」，更没有「Ø6 是因为要过 M6 螺丝」「这四个孔是一组阵列」「这面墙 2 mm 是因为要打印」。文件里每个面、每条边、每个点的名字都是空的。意图在代码里，在名字、常量和注释里。B-rep 是代码跑完之后剩下的东西。\n\n大家最先想到的理由是体积，其实它没有上面四条要紧。在我们的 28 个生成器上，STEP 的 token 数中位数是 model.py 的 22 倍；但简单的车削件或方块件只有 3 到 9 倍，法兰盘的 OCCT 原生 B-rep 格式和它的代码差不多长。比值是在几何重复的地方爆掉的：齿轮、Gridfinity 底板把每个齿、每个格子都完整写一遍，代码里只是一个循环。\n\n## 别人走过的路\n\n学界两条路都试过。最早的 CAD 生成模型之一 DeepCAD（2021）是有意选了命令序列而不是 B-rep：「命令序列很容易转成 B-rep，反过来却很难，因为不同的命令序列可能得到同一个 B-rep。」\n\n直接生成 B-rep 的模型，都把「有效率」当头号指标：\n\n- SolidGen 是一个用指针写顶点、边、面的 Transformer。它只支持直线、圆弧和几种简单曲面，只在 8 到 130 个面的零件上训练，报告的有效实体比例是 83% 到 88%。\n- BrepGen 是扩散模型，靠合并重复节点来恢复拓扑，再用 OpenCascade 把面缝起来。它报告 62.9%，并直说自己「不保证生成封闭的实体」。\n- 更新的模型在小零件上做得很好，零件一大就往下掉。有一篇报告 10 个面以内有效率 99%，21 到 30 个面时降到 74.5%。\n- 2026 年有一篇让语言模型直接写 STEP 文本的论文，只用 500 个实体以下的文件训练（我们那块板有 434 个），理由是「实体顺序或编号上的小错，就能让整份文件失效」。\n\n最清楚的例子是一家公司。Zoo 在 2023 年 12 月推出 Text-to-CAD 时，生成的是 B-rep 曲面，下载下来是 STEP。按他们 2026 年 2 月自己的说法：「我们最初走的是直接从文本生成 B-rep 的路子……有两件事最终让我们改了方向」，一是他们自己的 CAD 语言 KCL，二是大语言模型的崛起。他们的 agent 现在只返回 KCL 代码，直接产出 STEP 的那几个 API 在 2026 年 8 月下线了。谈到生成网格的模型时，他们一句话说完了整个道理：「这就像拿编译产物而不是源代码来训练。」\n\n也有人持相反意见。Autodesk 的 Mike Haley 说，他们的 neural CAD 模型「直接在几何里推理，而不是通过 API 去谈论几何」，并且把反对写代码的理由说得很直白：「让 LLM 去调参数化 CAD 的 API，就像雇一个读遍了所有管道书、却从没碰过一根水管的水管工。」这个诊断我们认，药方不认。只写代码、从不看结果的模型，确实就是那个水管工；我们的 agent 每改一次都要构建，再把内核建出来的东西读回来。\n\n## B-rep 该待的地方\n\n上面这些都不是在反对 B-rep。它就是我们交付的东西：你从 Forgent3D 下载的每一份 STEP，都是内核建出来的 B-rep。它也是 agent 检查自己工作的依据：每次构建之后，agent 读一份从 B-rep 算出来的报告，每个圆柱面的半径和轴线位置、每个平面所在的位置（[具体怎么做](/zh/blog/agent-that-measures)）。模型读的是 B-rep 的摘要，从来不是 B-rep 本身。\n\nB-rep 也是很好的输入。把 B-rep 当成一张由面和边组成的图来读的神经网络，用来给零件分类、给面打标签，效果很好。被图网络读，和被序列模型写，是两件不同的事。\n\n## 那模型该写什么？\n\n写交给内核编译的代码：短到人能读，用名字而不是指针，每个数只写一次，几何自洽这件事交给内核。具体是什么样的代码，除了几何还要带上什么，是另一个问题，也就是[下一篇](/zh/blog/cad-ir)。\n" },
  { slug: "cad-ir", locale: "en", file: "cad-ir.en.md", raw: "---\ntitle: A CAD IR for language models should not be a feature tree\ndescription: If the model should not write B-rep, what should it write? The layer between the language model and the kernel, a CAD IR. What that layer has to carry, how Zoo's KCL answers it, and why a feature tree written as text is not enough.\ndate: 2026-10-22\n---\n\n[The previous article](/en/blog/brep-not-for-transformers) ended on code that a CAD kernel compiles. That puts a layer between the language model and the kernel: the model writes it, the kernel turns it into B-rep, and a person reads and edits it, by hand or through an interface. Compilers call this kind of layer an intermediate representation. For CAD, call it a CAD IR.\n\nThe hard part is serving three readers at once. B-rep serves only the kernel. This article is about what the layer has to carry to serve all three, and why the most serious answer so far, Zoo's KCL, stops one level too low.\n\n## What the layer has to do\n\nBuilding Forgent3D taught us seven requirements.\n\n**Short, and written in order.** A transformer writes left to right, and every token is another chance to go wrong. Across our 28 generators the code is a median of 2,768 tokens; their STEP files are a median of 63,746.\n\n**Names instead of pointers.** `lid`, `boss`, `HOLE_D`. A name that does not exist fails on the line that used it.\n\n**Each number written once, along with where it came from.** In our models every constant says whether the user stated it, it was derived, or it is a default:\n\n```python\nBORE_D = 9.0   # stated: bearing bore (mm)\nWALL = 2.0     # default: printed wall (mm)\n```\n\nAfter the build we look for every stated number in the geometry: `BORE_D / 2` should show up as a cylinder of radius 4.5, and the report says whether it does. When someone drags a value in the interface, the edit is solved back into the constant it came from, and the formula around it stays a formula.\n\n**Refer to geometry by meaning.** The top face, the edge where the rib meets the base, the feature called bores; not face 7. Parametric CAD has a name for what happens otherwise, the topological naming problem: an edit upstream renumbers the faces and a fillet lands on the wrong edge. Zoo met it from the other side, when their own engine changed how it splits faces: \"If your CAD model has applied a fillet to edge #17, but that face is now edge #18, this new algorithm just changed which fillets were applied, breaking our stability promise.\" Our selectors rank references by how well they survive: a named feature first, then a query that matches exactly one thing, then an explicit ordering, and last of all the default order.\n\n**Compile the same way every time, and fail out loud.** The same source gives the same solid in your browser and on our server. If a fillet does not fit, the build says so and says where; it is never quietly made smaller.\n\n**Trace both ways.** Click a face and see the line that made it. Drag a number and see which constant it came from. That is what lets an interface and an agent point at the same thing.\n\n**Something the model can already write.** Language models have read an enormous amount of Python, though much less CAD: in one 2025 study, off-the-shelf models' CadQuery failed to build at least 47% of the time, and fine-tuning brought that under 2%. A new language starts from documentation and examples alone.\n\n## KCL, the most serious answer so far\n\nZoo, formerly KittyCAD, built a language for exactly this layer: KCL. Its premise is ours. In their words, \"All artifacts—including parts and assemblies—should be represented as human-readable code\", and \"All GUI (or point-and-click) interactions should be actions performed on this code representation under the hood.\" Every click in Zoo Design Studio writes KCL, and their agent, Zookeeper, edits the same KCL. The interface and the agent share one source, which is the property the list is after.\n\nAgainst the list, KCL does well where Python cannot. Variables cannot be reassigned. Arguments are named. Units are part of a number (`42mm`), with a default per file. References to edges are moving from tags to the faces around the edge, which, Zoo writes, \"makes references more stable when earlier modeling operations change the body's topology.\" The language and the app are open source (MIT); the interpreter is Rust compiled to WebAssembly.\n\nHere is a bracket profile in KCL 3.0, the version that became the default in October 2026, taken from Zoo's migration guide:\n\n```\nbracketProfile = sketch(on = XY) {\n  bottom = line(start = [var 0mm, var 0mm], end = [var 30mm, var 0mm])\n  right = line(start = [var 30mm, var 0mm], end = [var 30mm, var 20mm])\n  top = line(start = [var 30mm, var 20mm], end = [var 0mm, var 20mm])\n  left = line(start = [var 0mm, var 20mm], end = [var 0mm, var 0mm])\n  coincident([bottom.end, right.start])\n  coincident([right.end, top.start])\n  coincident([top.end, left.start])\n  coincident([left.end, bottom.start])\n  horizontal(bottom)\n  vertical(right)\n  horizontal(top)\n  vertical(left)\n}\nbracket = extrude(region(point = [15mm, 10mm], sketch = bracketProfile), length = 5mm)\n```\n\nA 30 × 20 × 5 mm plate: four segments, four coincidences, four orientation constraints, a region and an extrusion. Every line is clear. Together they are what parametric CAD has always stored, a feature tree. Zoo says as much: their roadmap describes the code editor as where \"you write the code that defines the model and feature tree 1:1\".\n\nThree costs come with it:\n\n- **The model has to learn it.** Nick Cameron, who worked on KCL's design, wrote: \"Since KCL is a new language there is not much code to train AI on.\" In August 2025 Zoo reported bringing their generation error rate down from 50% to 16%.\n- **It moves.** KCL 1.0 shipped in May 2025, 2.0 became the default in May 2026 and 3.0 in October 2026, and the pipeline style KCL was first written in is now deprecated. Old files keep working because they pin their version, but what a model learned last year is no longer what the documentation recommends.\n- **The geometry comes from Zoo's engine,** which is closed and runs in their cloud or on an enterprise's own servers. Without a connection there is no model.\n\n## A feature tree in text is not enough\n\nNX and CATIA have stored feature trees for decades, on kernels (Parasolid, CGM) hardened by decades of industrial parts. Writing the tree as text makes it diffable and gives a language model something to write. It does not change what the model has to think in: segments, constraints, which edge gets the fillet.\n\nThat is not how people ask for parts. They ask for a lid that snaps shut, four holes for M3 heat-set inserts, a case for this board with the USB port open. We see our agent go wrong in the translation from one to the other: reading a hand sketch the wrong way round, or working out from scratch a number that comes from printing experience, like the hole for a heat-set insert. A CAD IR for a language model should carry what a feature tree never stored:\n\n- **what each feature is for**: a clearance hole for M3, a boss for a heat-set insert, a snap fit, not a cylinder subtracted at some coordinates;\n- **which numbers the user stated** and which are defaults, so the build can check that what was asked for is what came out;\n- **requirements**: a minimum wall, a fit clearance, the printer bed the part has to fit on;\n- **relationships between parts**: this lid slides in that groove with 0.2 mm to spare;\n- **references to design objects**: the lid's mating face, the boss for the third screw, not faces and edges.\n\n## Where we are\n\nOur models are Python, in the style of build123d. Python is the \"something the model can already write\" on the list, and we lay part of the rest over it by convention:\n\n- a component library, so a heat-set insert boss is one call with the numbers already worked out instead of a cylinder the model derives each time ([why](/en/blog/components-catalog));\n- `stated` / `derived` / `default` on every constant, checked after the build;\n- markers that turn constants into sliders on the page;\n- selectors ranked by how well they survive an edit;\n- a build report the agent reads after every change ([how](/en/blog/agent-that-measures)).\n\nIt is convention, and Python enforces none of it. A model can still put a hole at absolute coordinates, pick an edge by its index, write the same number in three places. The build catches some of that afterwards. The language catches none of it beforehand.\n\nSo neither answer is the IR the list describes. KCL put its rules in the language, at the level of the feature tree. We put ours above the feature tree, in conventions the language knows nothing about.\n\nB-rep serves the kernel. A feature tree in text serves the kernel and the editor. A CAD IR for language models has to serve the model as well, in the terms people use when they ask for a part.\n\nHow KCL, build123d and the other options actually fare when a model writes them is in [Which CAD code should AI write?](/en/blog/ai-cad-languages)\n" },
  { slug: "cad-ir", locale: "zh", file: "cad-ir.zh.md", raw: "---\ntitle: 给语言模型用的 CAD IR，不该是一棵特征树\ndescription: 模型不该写 B-rep，那该写什么？语言模型和内核之间的那一层：CAD IR。这篇讲这一层要带上什么，Zoo 的 KCL 是怎么回答的，以及为什么把特征树写成文本还不够。\ndate: 2026-10-22\n---\n\n[上一篇](/zh/blog/brep-not-for-transformers)的结论是：让模型写交给 CAD 内核编译的代码。这样一来，语言模型和内核之间就多了一层：模型写它，内核把它变成 B-rep，人读它、改它，或者亲自动手，或者通过界面。编译器管这种东西叫中间表示（IR），放在 CAD 里，就叫 CAD IR。\n\n难处在于它要同时服务三个读者。B-rep 只服务内核。这篇讲这一层要带上什么才能服务三方，以及为什么目前最认真的答案，Zoo 的 KCL，停在了低一层的位置。\n\n## 这一层要做到什么\n\n做 Forgent3D 这段时间，我们攒下了七条。\n\n**短，按顺序写。** Transformer 从左往右写，每多一个 token 就多一次出错的机会。在我们的 28 个生成器上，代码的中位数是 2768 个 token，对应的 STEP 文件中位数是 63746 个。\n\n**用名字，不用指针。** `lid`、`boss`、`HOLE_D`。名字不存在，构建就停在用到它的那一行。\n\n**每个数只写一次，并写明从哪来。** 我们的模型里，每个常量都标着它是用户给的、推出来的，还是默认值：\n\n```python\nBORE_D = 9.0   # stated: bearing bore (mm)\nWALL = 2.0     # default: printed wall (mm)\n```\n\n构建之后，我们在几何里逐个找用户给的数：`BORE_D / 2` 应该表现为一个半径 4.5 的圆柱面，报告会说它在不在。有人在界面上拖动一个值，这次改动会沿算式反解回它来自的那个常量，周围的公式还是公式。\n\n**按意义引用几何。** 顶面、筋和底板相交的那条边、叫 bores 的那个特征，而不是「第 7 个面」。参数化 CAD 给反过来的情形起过名字，叫拓扑命名问题：上游一改，面重新编号，圆角落到了错的边上。Zoo 是从另一头撞上它的，在他们自己的引擎改了拆面的方式之后：「如果你的模型给第 17 条边倒了圆角，而那个面现在成了第 18 条边，这个新算法就改变了哪些边被倒圆角，打破了我们对稳定性的承诺。」我们的选择器按能不能扛住修改给引用排序：具名特征最稳，其次是恰好只命中一个的查询，再次是显式排序，最后才是默认顺序。\n\n**每次编译结果一样，失败要响。** 同一份源码，在你的浏览器里和在我们的服务器上建出同一个实体。圆角放不下，构建会说出来、说在哪里，绝不悄悄改小。\n\n**两个方向都能追溯。** 点一个面，看到建它的那一行；拖一个数，看到它来自哪个常量。界面和 agent 能指着同一样东西说话，靠的就是这个。\n\n**模型本来就会写。** 语言模型读过海量的 Python，CAD 代码却读得少得多：2025 年的一项研究里，现成的大模型写 CadQuery，至少有 47% 建不出有效的零件，专门微调之后降到 2% 以下。一门新语言，只能从文档和例子学起。\n\n## KCL，目前最认真的答案\n\nZoo（原来叫 KittyCAD）专门为这一层做了一门语言：KCL。它的前提和我们一样，用他们的话说：「所有产物，包括零件和装配，都应该表示成人能读的代码」，「所有图形界面上的点选操作，底层都应该是对这份代码的操作」。在 Zoo Design Studio 里每点一下都在写 KCL，他们的 agent Zookeeper 改的也是同一份 KCL。界面和 agent 共用一份源码，这正是上面那张清单想要的性质。\n\n对照清单，KCL 有几处是 Python 做不到的。变量不能重新赋值。参数要写名字。单位是数字的一部分（`42mm`），每个文件有默认单位。对边的引用正在从 tag 改成「用边两侧的面来指定」，Zoo 说这样「在前面的建模操作改变了实体拓扑时，引用更稳」。语言和应用都开源（MIT），解释器用 Rust 写成，编译为 WebAssembly。\n\n下面是一个支架轮廓的 KCL 3.0 写法（2026 年 10 月起成为默认版本），摘自 Zoo 的迁移指南：\n\n```\nbracketProfile = sketch(on = XY) {\n  bottom = line(start = [var 0mm, var 0mm], end = [var 30mm, var 0mm])\n  right = line(start = [var 30mm, var 0mm], end = [var 30mm, var 20mm])\n  top = line(start = [var 30mm, var 20mm], end = [var 0mm, var 20mm])\n  left = line(start = [var 0mm, var 20mm], end = [var 0mm, var 0mm])\n  coincident([bottom.end, right.start])\n  coincident([right.end, top.start])\n  coincident([top.end, left.start])\n  coincident([left.end, bottom.start])\n  horizontal(bottom)\n  vertical(right)\n  horizontal(top)\n  vertical(left)\n}\nbracket = extrude(region(point = [15mm, 10mm], sketch = bracketProfile), length = 5mm)\n```\n\n一块 30 × 20 × 5 mm 的板：四条线段、四个重合约束、四个水平或竖直约束，一个 region，一次拉伸。每一行都清楚，合起来就是参数化 CAD 一直在存的东西：一棵特征树。Zoo 自己也这么说，他们的路线图把代码编辑器描述成「你写下定义模型和特征树的代码，1:1」的地方。\n\n随之而来有三个代价：\n\n- **模型得现学。** 参与过 KCL 设计的 Nick Cameron 写过：「KCL 是一门新语言，能拿来训练 AI 的代码不多。」2025 年 8 月，Zoo 说他们把生成的出错率从 50% 降到了 16%。\n- **它在变。** KCL 1.0 在 2025 年 5 月发布，2.0 在 2026 年 5 月成为默认，3.0 在 2026 年 10 月；KCL 最早的管道写法已经废弃。旧文件钉住了版本，照样能跑，但模型去年学会的写法，已经不是文档现在推荐的写法。\n- **几何来自 Zoo 的引擎。** 它闭源，跑在 Zoo 的云上，或者企业自己的服务器上，断了网就建不了模。\n\n## 把特征树写成文本还不够\n\nNX 和 CATIA 存特征树已经几十年了，内核（Parasolid、CGM）被几十年的工业零件磨过。把特征树写成文本，能 diff，也让语言模型有东西可写，但模型要想的东西没变：线段、约束、圆角落在哪条边。\n\n人们要零件，不是这么说的。他们说：一个能扣上的盖子，四个放 M3 热熔螺母的孔，给这块板做个外壳、USB 口露出来。我们看到 agent 出错，就出在这两者之间的翻译上：把手绘草图的方向读反，或者从头去推一个本该来自打印经验的数，比如热熔螺母的孔径。给语言模型用的 CAD IR，应该带上特征树从来不存的东西：\n\n- **每个特征是干什么的**：M3 的过孔、热熔螺母柱、一个卡扣，而不是「在某个坐标减掉一个圆柱」；\n- **哪些数是用户给的**，哪些是默认值，这样构建才能核对要的东西是不是就是出来的东西；\n- **需求**：最小壁厚、配合间隙、零件要放得进的那块打印床；\n- **零件之间的关系**：这个盖子在那条槽里滑动，留 0.2 mm 余量；\n- **对设计对象的引用**：盖子的配合面、第三颗螺丝的柱，而不是面和边。\n\n## 我们现在在哪\n\n我们的模型是 Python，按 build123d 的风格写。清单上「模型本来就会写」那一条，Python 占了；其余的，我们靠约定往上叠了一部分：\n\n- 组件库：一个热熔螺母柱是一次调用，数字都已经算好，而不是每次让模型从头推一个圆柱（[为什么](/zh/blog/components-catalog)）；\n- 每个常量标 `stated` / `derived` / `default`，构建后核对；\n- 把常量变成页面滑块的标记；\n- 按能不能扛住修改来排序的选择器；\n- agent 每改一次都要读的构建报告（[怎么做的](/zh/blog/agent-that-measures)）。\n\n这些都是约定，Python 一条也不强制。模型照样可以把孔写在绝对坐标上、按序号挑一条边、把同一个数写在三个地方。构建能在事后抓到一部分，语言在事前一条也抓不到。\n\n所以这两个答案，都还不是清单描述的那个 IR。KCL 把规则写进了语言，但停在特征树那一层。我们把规则放在特征树之上，却只是语言本身不知道的约定。\n\nB-rep 服务内核。写成文本的特征树服务内核和编辑器。给语言模型用的 CAD IR，还得服务模型本身，而且要用人们要零件时说的那套话。\n\nKCL、build123d 等几种写法在模型手里实际表现如何，见[《AI 写 CAD 该用哪种代码》](/zh/blog/ai-cad-languages)。\n" },
  { slug: "code-is-model", locale: "en", file: "code-is-model.en.md", raw: "---\ntitle: Code is the model: why we have the AI write CAD code instead of generating a mesh\ndescription: There are two ways to do AI CAD, a mesh from a sentence or parametric code from a language model. We took the second, and this is why, and what it costs.\ndate: 2026-07-22\n---\n\nThe first decision in building Forgent3D was not which model to use. It was what the AI should produce.\n\nTwo roads. One is mesh generation: you say \"a mug with a handle\", and seconds later there is a pile of triangles that looks about right. The other is to have a language model write CAD code: a piece of Python that describes a cylinder, cuts a hole, rounds an edge; the geometry only exists once the code has run.\n\nWe took the second road. Not because it is more elegant, but because the first one produces something that is useless for what we wanted to do.\n\n## A mesh's problem is not precision, it is that it does not know what it is\n\nA hole in a mesh is not a hole. It is a ring of thin triangles arranged in a circle. You cannot ask it for its diameter; it does not know. You want to change that hole from 6 mm to 6.4 mm so an M6 screw passes through, and there is no \"change\" operation, only \"generate again\", and what comes back is a different mug with the handle somewhere else.\n\nA 3D printer does not care; it only wants triangles. But past the printer, nothing accepts a mesh: not CNC machining, not injection moulding, not opening the part in Fusion or SolidWorks to adjust it, not a quote from a machine shop. Let alone diffing two versions to see what changed.\n\n## The code is the single source of truth\n\nSo we made one rule: a project has one source of truth, `model.py`. It holds the geometry, the identity of every body, the named features, the default appearance; the assembly relations are in there too. Everything else, the B-rep, the mesh, the thumbnail, the scene the viewer draws, is derived from it and can be deleted and rebuilt at any time.\n\nThe consequence is that a model goes into git, can be reviewed, can be diffed, can be opened ten months later to change one number and rebuild. When the AI gets something wrong, you edit its code instead of rolling the dice again.\n\n## Change a number without destroying the formula\n\nOne thing we spent real effort on: letting a user change a dimension from the interface without breaking the intent in the code. Code the AI writes often looks like this:\n\n```python\nSTOCK = 20\nCLEAR = 1.5\nPLATE_H = STOCK - 2*CLEAR\nplate = Body(\"plate\", geometry=extrude(profile, amount=PLATE_H))\n```\n\nThe user sets the thickness to 15 in the panel. The lazy fix is to replace `PLATE_H` with a literal `15`, but then the formula is gone; it no longer follows `STOCK` and `CLEAR`, and the next time the stock changes the plate stays put. Keeping a separate \"UI parameter table\" with 15 in it creates a second source of truth: the code says one number, the panel another.\n\nWhat we do instead is change the value and keep the formula. The expression the interpreter recorded is solved backwards: 15 = STOCK − 2 × 1.5, so `STOCK` becomes 18 and the `PLATE_H` line is not touched. When it cannot be solved, two parameters sharing one number for instance, it fails with a message rather than guessing. Extrude depths, sketch dimensions and a helper's arguments all go through the same path.\n\n## The cost\n\nThe cost is plain: a language model writing code makes mistakes, and a geometric mistake is invisible in the code. A hole placed at 35 instead of 40 runs fine and looks plausible.\n\nSo the agent cannot just write code. It has to verify its own work: build after every change, measure, look at the render, and compare with what was asked. That is another article. The short version is that this verification is only possible on the \"code is the model\" road, because what gets measured is real geometry, not a heap of triangles.\n\nThe slogan came later: code is the model. The troubles above came first.\n" },
  { slug: "code-is-model", locale: "zh", file: "code-is-model.zh.md", raw: "---\ntitle: 代码就是模型：为什么我们让 AI 写 CAD 代码，而不是直接生成网格\ndescription: 做 AI CAD 有两条路，一句话出一个网格，或者让模型写参数化代码。我们选了后者，这篇讲为什么，以及代价是什么。\ndate: 2026-07-22\n---\n\n做 Forgent3D 的第一个决定不是用哪个模型，是 AI 生成的东西到底是什么。\n\n两条路摆在面前。一条是生成网格：你说「一个带把手的杯子」，出来一堆三角面，几秒钟，看起来像那么回事。另一条是让语言模型写 CAD 代码：它写的是一段 Python，描述一个圆柱、挖一个孔、倒一圈圆角，代码跑一遍，几何才出来。\n\n我们选了第二条。不是因为它更酷，是因为第一条做出来的东西没法用在我们想做的事上。\n\n## 网格的问题不是精度，是它不知道自己是什么\n\n一个网格里的孔不是孔，是一圈排成圆的窄长三角形。你没法问它「这个孔直径多少」，它不知道。你想把孔从 6 mm 改成 6.4 mm 好让 M6 螺丝穿过去，没有「改」这个操作，只能重新生成，而重新生成出来的是另一个杯子，把手的位置都不一样了。\n\n打印机不在乎这些，它只要三角面。但凡过了打印机这一关，CNC 加工、注塑、让 Fusion 或 SolidWorks 打开再改一改、给加工厂报价，没有一样接受网格。更不用说两个版本之间 diff 一下看改了什么。\n\n## 代码是唯一的真源\n\n所以我们定了一条规矩：每个项目只有一份真源，`model.py`。它里面有几何、有每个零件的身份、有命名的特征、有默认外观，装配关系也在里面。别的东西，B-rep、网格、缩略图、给查看器用的场景，全是从它算出来的派生品，随时可以删掉重建。\n\n这条规矩的后果是：模型可以放进 git，可以 review，可以 diff，可以在十个月后打开改一个数字然后重建。AI 写错了，你改它的代码，而不是重新抽卡。\n\n## 改一个数，但别砸掉公式\n\n有一件事我们花了不少力气，就是让用户从界面上改尺寸、但不破坏代码里的意图。AI 写的代码常常长这样：\n\n```python\nSTOCK = 20\nCLEAR = 1.5\nPLATE_H = STOCK - 2*CLEAR\nplate = Body(\"plate\", geometry=extrude(profile, amount=PLATE_H))\n```\n\n用户在面板上把厚度改成 15。最省事的做法是把 `PLATE_H` 直接写成 `15`，但这样公式没了，它和 `STOCK`、`CLEAR` 脱钩，下次改毛坯厚度板就不跟着动了。另起一张「界面参数表」存 15，又多了一份真源，代码说一个数、面板说另一个数。\n\n我们的做法是改值、保公式：从解释器记下来的表达式反解，15 = STOCK − 2 × 1.5，所以 `STOCK` 从 20 改成 18，`PLATE_H` 那一行一个字不动。两个参数共用同一个数、解不出来的，直接报错，不猜。拉伸深度、草图尺寸、一个 helper 的实参，改值走的都是这一条轨道。\n\n## 代价\n\n代价很明显：语言模型写代码会错，而几何上的错你从代码里看不出来。一个孔的位置写成了 35 而不是 40，代码照样跑，看起来也像那么回事。\n\n所以 agent 不能只写代码，它得自己验证：每改一次就构建一次，量尺寸，看截图，和要求对。这是另一篇的内容。但先说结论：验证这件事，恰恰只有「代码是模型」这条路才做得到，因为能量的是真实几何，而不是一堆三角面。\n\n口号是后来才有的：代码就是模型。先有的是上面那些麻烦。\n" },
  { slug: "components-catalog", locale: "en", file: "components-catalog.en.md", raw: "---\ntitle: Letting the AI reinvent the heat-set insert boss every time is a bad idea\ndescription: When the agent makes a box, ten times out of ten it needs insert bosses on the base and a catch on the lid, and it derives the numbers from scratch each time. One wrong number is one failed print. So we made the most common 3D-print joints into a library.\ndate: 2026-10-09\nupdated: 2026-10-21\n---\n\nRead enough agent session logs and one thing stands out: most of what it makes is not \"a part\", it is \"a part plus a few small structures that let it be assembled\". A box needs a lid, and the lid has to snap or catch. A base plate needs insert bosses, a PCB needs standoffs, a case needs a USB cutout and vent slots in its side, two panels need to turn on a pin.\n\nThese small structures have two properties. First, their geometry is easy; a heat-set insert boss is a cylinder with a hole. Second, their numbers are hard: whether the hole for an M3 heat-set insert should be 4.0 or 4.2, how thick the boss wall must be so the heat does not split it, how long the cantilever of a snap clip and how tall its barb so it both holds and releases. Those numbers come from printing experience, not from geometric intuition.\n\nA language model derives them from scratch every time. It derives them confidently, and differently each time. One wrong number is one failed print for the user.\n\n## So we made a library\n\nThe component library now holds twenty of the most common 3D-printed structures, roughly in groups:\n\n- Fastening: screw holes, heat-set insert bosses, nut traps, a printable bolt and nut.\n- Lids: a box with a lip lid, a box with a snap lid.\n- Openings: cable slots, port cutouts in standard connector sizes, vent grilles.\n- Motion: a pin hinge, a print-in-place hinge, a pin-and-bore pivot, a ball-and-socket joint, a dovetail slide.\n- Locating and retaining: peg-and-socket registration, snap clips, snap pins, magnet pockets, a bearing seat, PCB standoffs.\n\nEach component is a piece of code in our dialect plus a description: what problem it solves, what each parameter means, which numbers are print-experience values. When the agent models, it can list the components, read how one is done, and use it in its own part instead of inventing one.\n\n## A component is a method, not a part\n\nWe got this wrong at first too. A \"part library\" sounds natural: hundreds of ready-made boxes, brackets and hooks, and the user picks one and changes the dimensions. But such a library never has the one you need, and the bigger it gets the harder it is to search.\n\nA component is different. A heat-set insert boss is not a part; it is \"how to put a heat-set insert boss on any part\". It gets composed into the user's part: four on a base plate, two on the inside wall of a case. Its value is that the method is right, the numbers are right, and they are right the same way in every part that uses it.\n\nSo the library stays small. Twenty common ones are worth more than two hundred rare ones.\n\n## The numbers are parameters, because printers differ\n\nThe numbers in the library are common experience values, not standards. The right hole for the same M3 insert differs by a few tenths of a millimetre between printers, materials and layer heights. So every such number is a parameter, with a default most people can use and a knob for when your printer disagrees. The agent changes it once and every use in the session follows.\n\nA side benefit: the components work on their own. Without the agent, open the component page, drag the parameters, export, print. Someone who only wants a box with a lid does not need to talk to any AI.\n\n## What we expect it to change\n\nMostly the kind of failure. A box with a lid used to fail at the lid not catching or the boss being too thin, errors the user only finds after printing. With the library those should largely stop, and the failures that remain go back to the part itself: a dimension misread, a shape misunderstood. Those, at least, are visible on screen.\n\nThe library will grow, but only with what is genuinely common.\n\n*Update, 21 October 2026:* the library has grown to 38 components in ten groups. The new ones are more kinds of snap fit, living hinges, quick-release parts (a bayonet, a side-release buckle, a cam lever, a toolbox latch) and aluminium-extrusion parts. The peg-and-socket registration listed above is now a fit setting of the pin-and-bore component.\n" },
  { slug: "components-catalog", locale: "zh", file: "components-catalog.zh.md", raw: "---\ntitle: 让 AI 每次重新发明热熔螺母柱，是个坏主意\ndescription: agent 做盒子，十次有十次要在底板上做螺母柱、在盖子上做卡扣，每次重新推导数字，错一次就是一次失败的打印。所以我们把 3D 打印最常用的连接件做成了库。\ndate: 2026-10-09\nupdated: 2026-10-21\n---\n\n看多了 agent 的会话记录，会注意到一件事：它做的大部分东西不是「一个零件」，是「一个零件，加上几个让它能装起来的小结构」。盒子要有盖，盖要能扣住或者卡住；底板要有螺母柱，PCB 要有铜柱位，外壳侧面要开 USB 口和通风孔，两块板要能绕一根销转。\n\n这些小结构有两个特点。第一，它们的几何不难，一个热熔螺母柱就是一个带孔的圆柱。第二，它们的数字很难：M3 热熔螺母的孔该是 4.0 还是 4.2，柱壁留多厚才不会在热熔时撑裂，卡扣的悬臂多长、倒钩多高才能既扣得住又拔得下来。这些数字来自打印经验，不来自几何直觉。\n\n语言模型每次都从头推导这些数字。它推导得很自信，而且每次推导的结果不一样。错一次，就是用户一次失败的打印。\n\n## 所以做成库\n\n组件库里现在有二十个 3D 打印最常用的结构，大致分几组：\n\n- 固定：螺丝孔、热熔螺母柱、螺母槽、可打印的螺栓和螺母。\n- 盖子：唇边盖的盒子、卡扣盖的盒子。\n- 开口：线缆槽、接口开孔（标准接插件的尺寸）、通风格栅。\n- 活动：销轴铰链、一体打印铰链、销与孔的转轴、球窝关节、燕尾滑轨。\n- 定位和扣合：定位销、卡扣、卡销、磁铁槽、轴承座、PCB 铜柱。\n\n每个组件是一段我们方言里的代码，加一份说明：它解决什么问题，参数各是什么意思，哪些数字是打印经验值。agent 建模时可以列出组件、读某一个的做法，然后把它用进自己的零件里，而不是自己想一个。\n\n## 组件是做法，不是零件\n\n这一点一开始我们也想错过。一个「零件库」听起来很自然：几百个现成的盒子、支架、挂钩，用户挑一个改尺寸。但那样的库永远差你要的那一个，而且越大越难找。\n\n组件不一样。热熔螺母柱不是一个零件，它是「怎么在任何一个零件上放一个热熔螺母柱」。它会被组合进用户的零件里：一个底板上放四个，一个外壳的内壁上放两个。它的价值在于做法对，数字对，而且在每一个用到它的零件里都一样对。\n\n所以库不会很大。二十个常用的，比两百个不常用的有用。\n\n## 数字是参数，因为打印机不一样\n\n库里的数字是常见的经验值，不是标准。同样一个 M3 热熔螺母，不同的打印机、不同的材料、不同的层高，合适的孔径差零点几毫米。所以每一个这样的数字都是参数，默认值是多数人能用的，你的打印机不一样就改它。agent 改一次，这个会话里所有用到它的地方跟着改。\n\n还有一个顺带的好处：这些组件自己就能用。不经过 agent，打开组件页，拖参数，导出，打印。一个只想要一个带盖盒子的人，不需要跟任何 AI 说话。\n\n## 我们期望它改变什么\n\n主要是失败的类型。以前 agent 做一个带盖盒子，失败在盖子扣不上、螺母柱太薄，这类错误用户要打印出来才发现。有了库，这类错误应该大体消失，剩下的失败回到零件本身：尺寸理解错了，形状想象错了。这些至少在屏幕上看得见。\n\n库会继续加，但只加真的常用的。\n\n*2026 年 10 月 21 日更新：* 组件库现在有十组、38 个组件，新加的是更多种卡扣、活铰链、快拆件（卡口、侧按插扣、凸轮快拆杆、工具箱搭扣）和铝型材件。上面列的定位销，现在是 pin-and-bore 组件里的一种配合选项。\n" },
  { slug: "eval-variance", locale: "en", file: "eval-variance.en.md", raw: "---\ntitle: We spent 13 hours proving our own evaluation was useless\ndescription: Eight experiment cells to answer \"was that prompt better\". No answer came back. What did: rerunning one configuration swings it by 16 points, while every difference we wanted to measure is under 10.\ndate: 2026-08-28\n---\n\nLate in August there was a concrete question: one day's agent evaluation report looked better than any recent one. Was that version of the prompt better? We set up a controlled comparison to find out.\n\nIt ran eight cells, about 13 hours. The question was not answered. This is why, and what we learned instead, which turned out to be more useful than the answer.\n\n## The ruler\n\nThe evaluation uses drawing cases: hand the agent an engineering drawing, let it build the part, then check the geometry it built against dimensions transcribed by hand from the drawing. The checking step is offline, no language model, no sandbox, no network; the code stored in the report is rebuilt on a local kernel and checked line by line.\n\nWe picked four cases, 43 dimension checks in all: a stepped shaft with 20, an elliptical cam with 7, a triple-boss rocker with 8, a carry handle with 8. Cases that scored 100% three times in a row were excluded (saturated, nothing to measure), and so were cases where the baseline side itself had errored. The prompt, reference image and configuration of these four cases were byte-for-byte identical between the two code versions; we checked.\n\n## Eight cells\n\nTwo code versions and four model channels, laid out as eight cells. Each cell runs once, ten cases per run.\n\nThe one line that matters first: **two of the cells were exactly the same configuration**, same version, same prompt, same channel, same cases, same ruler, just run twice. One scored 62.8%, the other 79.1%. A difference of 16.3 points; the number of wrong checks fell from 16 to 8, half.\n\nThen the things we had meant to compare:\n\n- old prompt versus new: 9.7 points apart.\n- channel A versus channel B: 9.3 points.\n- channel B versus channel C: 9.3 points.\n- old code versus new: 7.9 points.\n\nNot one of those exceeds 16.3. So this experiment could say nothing about the prompt, the code or the channel. The report that looked better had most likely just landed on the good side of the swing.\n\n## Pass rates are worse than dimensions\n\nWe started with pass/fail and only later switched to per-dimension checks. The reason: the \"better looking\" report had been an interrupted run, five of ten cases unfinished, 8/10 once the missing ones were rerun. Checking out the whole old tree and rerunning the same ten cases gave 10/10. Neither of the original two failures reproduced; one of them had failed because of the interruption itself.\n\nThe noise at the pass/fail level is such that one interruption rewrites the conclusion.\n\n## The ruler is blind sometimes\n\nPer-dimension checks are not clean either. The scoring script has a comment in capitals that says, in effect: a cylinder check only works if the face is actually a cylinder. Four things turn a diameter on the drawing into something the ruler cannot read: a draft angle turns the cylinder into a cone, a loft or sweep turns it into a spline surface, a fillet on a round edge turns it into a torus, and the reader can simply throw. All four **look exactly like \"the feature was never built\"** in the data.\n\nSo every \"wrong\" has to be sorted into three bins before it means anything: the model was wrong, the ruler was blind, the environment broke. Without the sorting, the count of wrong checks is a mix of three different things and cannot be compared.\n\n## The one lead\n\nOne single-channel cell scored 93.0%, the same as the good-looking report, with only 3 wrong checks and 19/20 on the stepped shaft, the best of the whole set. So the good report needed no \"three channels rotating\" explanation; one channel was enough. But that cell also ran once, and is statistically indistinguishable from the channel that swung 16 points. Confirming it means repeating it three to five times.\n\n## How to run it next time\n\n- Measure the variance before talking about effects. Run the same configuration at least three times, learn how much it swings by itself, and only then decide which differences count.\n- Cells with n = 1 cannot be compared. This is the one conclusion of the whole experiment that a repeated sample directly verified.\n- Sort the errors. Model wrong, ruler blind, environment broken; count them separately.\n- Do not change the environment while the evaluation runs. In those thirteen hours we dug up three environment traps and one defect in the channel protocol on the side, each of them enough to contaminate a result.\n\nThis was written down not to keep a conclusion, but to keep \"do not run it like this again\".\n" },
  { slug: "eval-variance", locale: "zh", file: "eval-variance.zh.md", raw: "---\ntitle: 我们花了 13 个小时，证明自己的评测没法用\ndescription: 为了回答「那版 prompt 是不是更好」跑了八组实验。答案没拿到，拿到的是：同一配置重跑一次就摆动 16 个点，而所有想比的差值都在 10 个点以内。\ndate: 2026-08-28\n---\n\n八月下旬有一个很具体的问题：某一天的 agent 评测报告看起来比最近的都好，是不是那一版 prompt 更好？我们决定做个对照实验，把它搞清楚。\n\n实验跑了八组，大约 13 个小时。问题没有被回答。这篇写的是为什么，以及我们从中学到的、比答案更有用的东西。\n\n## 尺子\n\n评测用的是图纸用例：给 agent 一张工程图，让它建出零件，然后拿人工从图纸上抄下来的尺寸去量它建出来的几何。量的那一步是离线的，不经过语言模型，也不经过沙箱和网络，就是把报告里存的代码在本地内核上重建一遍，再逐条检查。\n\n我们选了四个用例，一共 43 条尺寸检查：一根台阶轴 20 条，一个椭圆凸轮 7 条，一个三凸台摇臂 8 条，一个提手 8 条。排除了三次全对的（饱和了，量不出差别）和基准侧本身就报错的。这四个用例的 prompt、参考图、配置在新旧两个版本之间逐字节相同，这一点专门校验过。\n\n## 八组\n\n两个代码版本，四个模型渠道，排列出八组。每组跑一遍，每遍十个用例。\n\n结果先放一行最要紧的：**有两组是完全相同的配置**，同一个版本、同一个 prompt、同一个渠道、同一批用例、同一把尺子，只是跑了两次。一次 62.8%，一次 79.1%。差 16.3 个点，错的条数从 16 减到 8，减了一半。\n\n然后看我们本来想比的东西：\n\n- prompt 新旧：差 9.7 个点。\n- 渠道 A 和渠道 B：差 9.3 个点。\n- 渠道 B 和渠道 C：差 9.3 个点。\n- 代码新旧：差 7.9 个点。\n\n没有一个差值超过 16.3。也就是说，这次实验对 prompt、对代码、对渠道，什么都判断不了。那个看起来更好的报告，很可能只是摇到了好的那一面。\n\n## 通过率比尺寸更不可靠\n\n一开始我们看的是通过 / 失败，后来才换成逐条尺寸。原因是那份「看起来更好」的报告本身是一次被中断的运行，十个用例里五个没跑完，补跑之后合计 8/10。把整棵旧代码树 checkout 回来，同样十个用例重跑一遍，10/10。原来那两个失败一个也没复现，其中一个的失败原因就是那次中断。\n\n通过率这层噪声太大，一个中断就能改写结论。\n\n## 尺子自己也会瞎\n\n逐条尺寸也不是干净的。打分脚本里有一段大写的注释，大意是：圆柱检查只有在那个面真的是圆柱的时候才有效。有四种情况会让图纸上的一个直径变成尺子读不出来的东西：拔模把圆柱变成了圆锥，放样或扫掠把它变成了样条面，圆边倒角把它变成了环面，还有读取器直接抛错。这四种在数据里**看起来都和「特征没建」一模一样**。\n\n所以每一个「错」在定性之前都要分三类：模型真错了，尺子瞎了，环境崩了。不分类，错的条数就是一个混合了三种东西的数，比不了。\n\n## 唯一的线索\n\n有一组单渠道拿到了 93.0%，和那份最好看的报告一样，错的只有 3 条，台阶轴 19/20 是全场最高。这说明那份好看的报告不需要「三个渠道轮换」来解释，一个渠道就够。但它也只跑了一次，和那个摆动 16 个点的渠道在统计上区分不开。要确认，得重复三到五次。\n\n## 下次怎么跑\n\n- 先量方差，再谈效应。同一配置至少跑三次，知道它自己摆多少，才知道什么差值算数。\n- n = 1 的格子之间不能比较。这一条是整个实验里唯一被重复样本直接验证的结论。\n- 错要分类。模型错、尺子瞎、环境崩，各记各的。\n- 不要在跑评测的时候改环境。我们在这十三个小时里顺手挖出了三个环境陷阱和一个渠道协议的缺陷，它们每一个都足以污染结果。\n\n这篇写下来的目的不是留结论，是留「下次别再这么跑」。\n" },
  { slug: "openscad-green-faces", locale: "en", file: "openscad-green-faces.en.md", raw: "---\ntitle: Green faces in the OpenSCAD preview, which color() wins, and what % and # actually draw\ndescription: The walls of a hole come out green and that is not a mistake. The preview's colour conventions, why the outer color() wins, and what happens to % and # in preview and render.\ndate: 2026-10-06\napp: viewer\n---\n\nAlmost everyone who uses OpenSCAD asks this at some point: I cut a hole with `difference()`, pressed F5, and the walls of the hole are green while everything else is yellow. Did I do something wrong?\n\nNo. That is the preview's convention, and it is deliberate.\n\n## What yellow and green mean\n\nThe default colour scheme is called Cornfield. Faces of the model are yellow, `#F9D72C`. Faces **left behind by a subtracted object** are green, `#9DCB51`. F5 renders with OpenCSG, an image-based CSG: every pixel on screen ends up belonging to the surface of one primitive. If that primitive was the one you subtracted, the pixel is drawn in the \"back face\" colour, so the walls of the hole are green.\n\nThe convention is useful when debugging. Green says \"this surface was cut out\", yellow says \"this surface was there to begin with\". Whether a hole goes all the way through, or whether two holes broke into each other, is faster to read from the colours than from rotating the model.\n\nPress F6 and everything turns yellow. That is normal too. The old CGAL render path kept no colours at all and drew every face the same. Development builds of the last two years use Manifold as the geometry kernel, and Manifold remembers for every triangle which original primitive it came from; the colour and the \"subtracted\" flag travel through the booleans with it, so recent builds show colours in F6 as well.\n\n## The rule for color(): the outer one wins\n\nThis one is counter-intuitive, but it is how it is defined:\n\n```\ncolor(\"red\") color(\"blue\") cube(10);\n```\n\nThat cube is red. Not blue.\n\nOpenSCAD walks the tree from the root downwards, records the first `color()` it meets, and ignores every `color()` below it. In the source it is one line: `if (!state.color().isValid()) state.setColor(node.color);`. So when a library module colours its own part and you wrap it in a `color()` of your own, yours replaces it. The other way round, colouring a group from the outside while keeping one part's own colour, cannot be done; you have to take that part out of the group.\n\nA subtracted object that carries its own `color()` leaves faces in that colour, not green. If you want blue walls in the hole, give the cylinder `color(\"blue\")`.\n\nColours can be written three ways: a name (the 147 SVG names, `\"red\"`, `\"steelblue\"`, `\"lightgray\"`, case does not matter), a hex string `\"#RRGGBB\"` or `\"#RRGGBBAA\"`, or a vector `[r, g, b]` or `[r, g, b, a]`. The vector components run from 0 to 1, not from 0 to 255. The fourth one is the alpha: `color(\"red\", 0.5)` and `color([1, 0, 0, 0.5])` both make a part translucent, so you can see what sits behind it in the preview.\n\n## % and #\n\n`%` is the background modifier. A subtree marked with `%` is **left out of the render**: after F6 it does not exist, and it is not in the exported STL. In the F5 preview it is drawn in translucent grey. The common use is to put the thing a part will be mounted on next to it, as a reference:\n\n```\n%import(\"case.stl\");       // the enclosure: look at it, do not build it\ntranslate([12, 8, 3]) cube([30, 20, 2]);   // the PCB tray being designed\n```\n\n`#` is the debug modifier. A subtree marked with `#` **is rendered as usual**, and in the preview it is additionally drawn in translucent pink. It answers \"where is this thing\". The cylinder you subtract inside a `difference()` is invisible by nature; put a `#` in front of it and it shows up in pink, and you can see at once whether it sits where you meant. In the render and in exports `#` does nothing.\n\nThe other two modifiers, `!` and `*`, are less common: `!` renders only that subtree and nothing else, `*` disables the subtree entirely. Both are dealt with during evaluation and have nothing to do with colours.\n\n## How our browser preview handles all this\n\nThe Forgent3D [online viewer](/en/openscad-viewer) runs the real OpenSCAD, so every point above carries over unchanged: yellow faces, green faces, the outer `color()` wins, `%` in translucent grey, `#` in translucent pink, `$preview` set to true. The one difference is that the preview geometry comes from Manifold's real booleans rather than OpenCSG's image compositing, so coincident faces do not flicker the way they do in F5. Colours exist only in the preview; the exported STEP carries none, just as the desktop's STL does not.\n" },
  { slug: "openscad-green-faces", locale: "zh", file: "openscad-green-faces.zh.md", raw: "---\ntitle: OpenSCAD 预览里的绿面、color() 的优先级，还有 % 和 # 到底画了什么\ndescription: 挖完孔孔壁是绿的，不是你写错了。这篇讲 OpenSCAD 预览的配色约定、color() 为什么外层赢、% 和 # 在预览和渲染里各是什么下场。\ndate: 2026-10-06\napp: viewer\n---\n\n用 OpenSCAD 的人几乎都问过同一个问题：`difference()` 挖了个孔，按 F5 一看，孔的内壁是绿的，别的地方是黄的。是不是哪里写错了？\n\n没有。这是 OpenSCAD 预览的约定，而且它是故意的。\n\n## 黄和绿各是什么\n\n默认的配色方案叫 Cornfield。模型的面是黄色 `#F9D72C`；**被减掉的物体留下的面**是绿色 `#9DCB51`。F5 用的是 OpenCSG，一种基于图像的 CSG：屏幕上每个像素最后都归属于某一个图元的表面。一个像素如果落在被 `difference()` 减掉的那个物体的表面上，就按「背面色」画，于是你看到的孔壁是绿的。\n\n这个约定在调试时很有用。绿面告诉你「这里是减出来的」，黄面告诉你「这里是原来就有的」。一个孔挖穿了没有、两个孔有没有打到一起，看颜色比转着看快得多。\n\n按 F6 渲染，画面变成一水的黄，这也是正常的。老的 CGAL 渲染路径根本不记颜色，所有面都用同一个颜色画。近两年的开发版换了 Manifold 做几何内核，它给每个三角面记着自己来自哪个原始图元，颜色和「被减」这个标记跟着布尔运算一起传下去，所以新版本的 F6 也能看到颜色。\n\n## color() 的规则：外层赢\n\n这一条反直觉，但它就是这么定的：\n\n```\ncolor(\"red\") color(\"blue\") cube(10);\n```\n\n这个方块是红的。不是蓝的。\n\nOpenSCAD 从树的根往下走，遇到第一个 `color()` 就把颜色记下来，往下再遇到的 `color()` 全部忽略。源码里就一行：`if (!state.color().isValid()) state.setColor(node.color);`。所以一个库里的 module 自己给零件上了色，你在外面再包一层 `color()`，你的颜色覆盖它的。反过来想在外层给一组零件统一上色、又想保留某个零件自己的颜色，做不到，只能把它拆出来。\n\n被减掉的物体自己带了 `color()`，它留下的面显示它自己的颜色，而不是绿色。要让孔壁是蓝的，给那个圆柱 `color(\"blue\")` 就行。\n\n颜色有三种写法：名字（SVG 那 147 个，`\"red\"`、`\"steelblue\"`、`\"lightgray\"` 都行，大小写不敏感）、十六进制 `\"#RRGGBB\"` 或 `\"#RRGGBBAA\"`、向量 `[r, g, b]` 或 `[r, g, b, a]`。向量的分量是 0 到 1，不是 0 到 255。第四个分量是透明度，`color(\"red\", 0.5)` 或者 `color([1, 0, 0, 0.5])` 都能让零件半透明，预览时能看到它背后的东西。\n\n## % 和 #\n\n`%` 叫背景修饰符。加了 `%` 的子树**不参与渲染**：F6 之后它不存在，导出的 STL 里也没有它。F5 预览时它画成半透明的灰色。最常见的用法是在设计一个零件时把它要装上去的东西摆在旁边当参照：\n\n```\n%import(\"case.stl\");       // 外壳，只看不算\ntranslate([12, 8, 3]) cube([30, 20, 2]);   // 真正在设计的 PCB 托\n```\n\n`#` 叫调试修饰符。加了 `#` 的子树**照常参与渲染**，另外在预览里再叠一层半透明的粉红色。它用来回答「这块到底在哪」。一个 `difference()` 里减去的圆柱看不见，前面加个 `#`，它就以粉红色显形，位置对不对一眼就清楚。渲染和导出的时候 `#` 没有任何作用。\n\n另外两个修饰符 `!` 和 `*` 比较少用：`!` 只渲染这一棵子树、别的全不要，`*` 把这棵子树整个禁掉。它们在求值阶段就处理掉了，和颜色无关。\n\n## 我们的浏览器预览怎么处理这些\n\nForgent3D 的[在线预览](/zh/openscad-viewer)跑的是真正的 OpenSCAD，所以上面每一条都照搬：黄面、绿面、外层赢、`%` 半透明灰、`#` 半透明粉、`$preview` 为 true。唯一的区别是预览的几何来自 Manifold 的真实布尔运算，不是 OpenCSG 的图像合成，所以两个物体的面重合时不会出现 F5 那种闪烁。颜色只存在于预览里；导出的 STEP 不带颜色，这一点和桌面版导出 STL 一样。\n" },
  { slug: "openscad-preview-online", locale: "en", file: "openscad-preview-online.en.md", raw: "---\ntitle: Preview OpenSCAD code online: no install, paste it and look\ndescription: Seeing what a piece of OpenSCAD code looks like does not require installing OpenSCAD. Paste it into a viewer in the browser, it renders when you pause, colours match the desktop, and the code can go into a link for someone else.\ndate: 2026-10-10\nupdated: 2026-10-21\napp: viewer\n---\n\nThe traditional way to see what a piece of OpenSCAD code draws is: download the installer, install it, new file, paste, press F5. On your own machine that is a few minutes. On someone else's machine, on a phone, or when you only want a look at the twenty lines ChatGPT just wrote, those minutes are in the way.\n\n[Forgent3D's OpenSCAD viewer](/en/openscad-viewer) exists for that one look.\n\n## How it works\n\nOpen the page and paste the code into the editor. There is nothing to click. Half a second after you stop typing the model appears beside it (above it on a phone). Change a number and it redraws half a second later.\n\nIf you have a file, drop the .scad in. If you have a whole project, drop the folder in; `include` and `use` find their files in the real directory layout, and the common libraries, MCAD, Write.scad, BOSL2, are built in. Parameters declared with Customizer comments at the top of the file show up as sliders in the parameters panel; drag one and the source follows.\n\n## What you see is what the desktop shows\n\nThe code is run by the real OpenSCAD, not something that imitates its syntax, so anything that runs on the desktop is read the same way here. The colours follow the desktop's conventions too: a part with `color()` shows its own colour, faces cut by `difference()` are green, anything marked `%` is drawn in translucent grey and anything marked `#` gets a translucent pink overlay.\n\nThe one difference is that it is closer to F6 than to F5: the geometry is actually computed, so two objects sharing a face do not flicker.\n\n## Put the code in a link\n\nHappy with the preview? Click \"Copy link\". The link carries the code you have open, and whoever opens it sees the rendered model without pasting anything.\n\nIt works the other way too. When you ask ChatGPT, Gemini or Claude for a piece of OpenSCAD, ask it for a Forgent3D preview link as well, of the form `https://app.forgent3d.com/scad#code=<code>`. Open it and the model is there. The link format is written out on the [viewer page](/en/openscad-viewer#open-from-link); copy it into the chat and the assistant will follow it.\n\n## The code never leaves your browser\n\nThe preview runs in your browser and the .scad files are not uploaded; what comes after `#` in a link is not sent to the server either.\n\n## On the side\n\nIf you want a file once the preview looks right, the same page exports STEP, real B-rep that Fusion, SolidWorks and FreeCAD open. That goes beyond a look; the details are on the [OpenSCAD to STEP](/en/openscad-to-step) page.\n\nNo code at hand? The viewer page has three ready examples: a flange, a pair of gears, a box with a lid. Open one and drag the parameters.\n" },
  { slug: "openscad-preview-online", locale: "zh", file: "openscad-preview-online.zh.md", raw: "---\ntitle: 在线预览 OpenSCAD 代码：不装软件，贴进去就能看\ndescription: 想看一段 OpenSCAD 代码长什么样，不一定要装 OpenSCAD。贴进浏览器里的预览器，停下来就渲染，颜色和桌面版一样，还能把代码放进链接里发给别人。\ndate: 2026-10-10\nupdated: 2026-10-21\napp: viewer\n---\n\n想看一段 OpenSCAD 代码画出来是什么，传统的路是：下载安装包，装上，新建文件，粘贴，按 F5。在自己的电脑上这是几分钟的事，但换一台电脑、用手机、或者只是想看一眼 ChatGPT 刚写的那二十行，这几分钟就显得多余了。\n\n[Forgent3D 的 OpenSCAD 在线预览](/zh/openscad-viewer)就是为这一眼做的。\n\n## 怎么用\n\n打开页面，把代码贴进编辑器，什么都不用点。停下输入半秒，模型就出现在旁边（手机上在上面）。改一个数，半秒后重画。\n\n有文件的话，把 .scad 文件拖进来；有一整个项目的话，把文件夹拖进来，`include` 和 `use` 按真实目录找文件，MCAD、Write.scad、BOSL2 这几个常用库已经内置。文件顶上写了 Customizer 注释的参数，会变成参数面板里的滑块，拖滑块改，源码跟着改。\n\n## 看到的和桌面版一样\n\n跑代码的是真正的 OpenSCAD，不是一个模仿它语法的东西，所以能在桌面版里运行的代码这里的理解完全一样。颜色也照着桌面版的约定来：`color()` 上过色的零件显示自己的颜色，被 `difference()` 减掉的面是绿的，`%` 的东西画成半透明灰，`#` 的东西叠一层半透明粉。\n\n唯一的差别是它比 F5 更接近 F6：几何是真算出来的，两个物体贴面的地方不会闪。\n\n## 把代码放进链接里\n\n预览满意了，点「复制链接」。那个链接里带着你当前的代码，谁点开，看到的就是渲染好的模型，不用再贴一遍。\n\n反过来也行。你让 ChatGPT、Gemini 或者 Claude 写一段 OpenSCAD，可以顺手让它给一个 Forgent3D 预览链接，形如 `https://app.forgent3d.com/scad#code=<代码>`。点开就是模型。链接格式在[预览页](/zh/openscad-viewer#open-from-link)上写着，直接复制给 AI 看就行。\n\n## 代码不会离开你的浏览器\n\n预览在你的浏览器里运行，.scad 文件不上传；链接里 `#` 后面的内容浏览器也不会发给服务器。\n\n## 顺手的事\n\n预览满意了要文件的话，同一个页面能导出 STEP，真正的 B-rep，Fusion、SolidWorks、FreeCAD 都能打开。这就超出「看一眼」的范围了，细节在 [OpenSCAD 转 STEP](/zh/openscad-to-step) 那一页。\n\n手边没有代码的话，预览页上有三个现成的例子：法兰、一对齿轮、带盖的盒子。点开就能拖参数。\n" },
  { slug: "openscad-to-step-guide", locale: "en", file: "openscad-to-step-guide.en.md", raw: "---\ntitle: How to export STEP from OpenSCAD: the usual ways, and the one we built\ndescription: OpenSCAD only exports meshes like STL, so getting a STEP file takes a detour. The FreeCAD workbench, mesh-to-STEP converters, remodelling by hand, each with its own cost. Which road fits which job, and an online converter that needs no install.\ndate: 2026-09-30\napp: step\n---\n\nThere is no STEP in OpenSCAD's export menu. That is not an omission, it is the design: OpenSCAD turns every model into triangles, and all it can export are mesh formats, STL, 3MF, OFF, AMF.\n\nA printer does not mind. But the moment a part goes to a CNC shop, to injection moulding, or into Fusion, SolidWorks or FreeCAD for further work, what they want is STEP. In a STEP file a hole is a cylindrical face with a diameter; in a mesh it is a ring of thin triangles that CAD cannot select, cannot measure, and CAM does not recognise.\n\nSo \"export STEP from OpenSCAD\" is a question that comes up on the forums every few months. Here are the usual answers.\n\n## The FreeCAD OpenSCAD workbench\n\nFreeCAD can import a .scad file, or the .csg that OpenSCAD exports, rebuild the primitives inside as solids, and export STEP. It is the oldest and most often recommended route.\n\nThe cost is that you need both FreeCAD and OpenSCAD installed, and it cannot rebuild everything. Operations like `hull()` and `minkowski()` often come back as meshes, so the STEP ends up half solid, half facets. Multi-file projects and files that use libraries usually need tidying by hand first.\n\nFor: simple parts, both programs already installed, and the patience to handle failures manually.\n\n## Mesh-to-STEP converters\n\nExport STL from OpenSCAD first, then \"wrap\" it in a STEP file with a converter. There are many, online and desktop.\n\nWhat they do is turn every triangle into a planar face. The file is a STEP and opens anywhere, but it is still a mesh: no cylinders, no holes to select, and thousands of tiny faces that can bring CAM software to its knees. Send one of these for a quote and the shop will probably ask for the \"real\" model.\n\nFor: when the other side only needs a file that opens and nobody will edit it.\n\n## Remodel it by hand\n\nOpen a CAD program and redraw the part from its dimensions. The result is exact and the cleanest of all.\n\nThe cost is time, hours per part, and every change to the .scad means doing it again. For something parametric, that amounts to giving up the parameters.\n\nFor: a one-time conversion of a simple part.\n\n## What we built: convert in the browser\n\n[Forgent3D's OpenSCAD to STEP](/en/openscad-to-step) takes another route. The real OpenSCAD executes your code, so the language behaves exactly as on your machine, modules, functions, include, use, MCAD and BOSL2 included; a CAD kernel then rebuilds every primitive and boolean as exact geometry, a circle becomes a true cylinder, and that is exported as STEP.\n\nUsing it means dropping a .scad file or a whole project folder in, looking at the preview, and clicking export. Nothing to install, no sign-up. Customizer parameters become sliders, so you can set the dimensions before exporting.\n\nIt has limits too: `hull()` and `minkowski()` are exact for common shapes and approximated, with a clear warning, where no exact form exists; `linear_extrude` with `twist` has no exact form; an STL brought in by `import()` stays faceted. All of it is listed on the [compatibility](/en/openscad-to-step/compatibility) page. We test against several thousand real files from Thingiverse, and most convert outright.\n\n## Which to pick\n\n- You only need a file that opens and nobody will edit it: a mesh-to-STEP converter is fastest.\n- It will be edited in CAD, sent to a shop, or machined: you need real B-rep, from the FreeCAD workbench or our converter.\n- A simple part, converted once in its life: redrawing works too.\n\nIf you have not decided, drop the file into the [converter](/en/openscad-to-step) and see. If it cannot, it tells you why.\n" },
  { slug: "openscad-to-step-guide", locale: "zh", file: "openscad-to-step-guide.zh.md", raw: "---\ntitle: OpenSCAD 怎么导出 STEP：现有的几种办法，和我们做的这个\ndescription: OpenSCAD 只能导出 STL 这类网格，要 STEP 得绕路。FreeCAD 工作台、网格转 STEP、照尺寸重画，各有各的代价。这篇讲每条路适合谁，以及一个不用安装的在线转换器。\ndate: 2026-09-30\napp: step\n---\n\nOpenSCAD 的「导出」菜单里没有 STEP。这不是漏了，是它的设计：OpenSCAD 把所有模型算成三角面，能导的只有 STL、3MF、OFF、AMF 这类网格格式。\n\n打印机不在乎。但只要你要把零件交给 CNC 加工厂、拿去注塑、或者在 Fusion、SolidWorks、FreeCAD 里继续改，对方要的都是 STEP。STEP 里的孔是一个有直径的圆柱面，网格里的孔是一圈窄长的三角形，CAD 软件选不中它、量不出它、CAM 认不出它。\n\n于是「OpenSCAD 导出 STEP」成了一个每隔几个月就有人在论坛上问一遍的问题。下面是几种常见的答案。\n\n## FreeCAD 的 OpenSCAD 工作台\n\nFreeCAD 能导入 .scad 文件，或者 OpenSCAD 导出的 .csg，把里面的图元重建成实体，再导出 STEP。这是最老、也最常被推荐的办法。\n\n代价是你得在本机装好 FreeCAD 和 OpenSCAD 两个软件，而且它不是什么都能重建。`hull()` 和 `minkowski()` 这类操作在很多情况下会以网格的形式导回来，最后的 STEP 一半是实体一半是面片。多文件项目、用了库的文件，常常要先手工整理一番。\n\n适合：零件不复杂、本机已经有这两个软件、愿意手动处理失败的人。\n\n## 网格转 STEP 的工具\n\n先从 OpenSCAD 导出 STL，再用一个转换工具把它「包」成 STEP。这类工具很多，在线的桌面的都有。\n\n它们做的事情是把每个三角形变成一个平面。文件确实是 STEP，哪里都打得开，但本质还是网格：没有圆柱，孔选不中，几千上万个小面能让 CAM 软件卡死。拿这种 STEP 去报价，加工厂多半会问你要「真正的」图。\n\n适合：对方只是要一个能打开的 STEP 文件、不需要在里面继续编辑的场合。\n\n## 照尺寸重画一遍\n\n打开 CAD 软件，按尺寸把零件重新画一遍。结果是精确的，也是最干净的。\n\n代价是时间：一个零件几小时，而且 .scad 一改，又得重来。对参数化的东西来说，这等于放弃了参数化。\n\n适合：只需要转这一次、零件很简单的情况。\n\n## 我们做的：在浏览器里直接转\n\n[Forgent3D 的 OpenSCAD 转 STEP](/zh/openscad-to-step) 走的是另一条路。执行代码的是 OpenSCAD 本体，所以语言行为和你本机一致，module、function、include、use、MCAD 和 BOSL2 都认；然后由一个 CAD 内核把每个图元和布尔运算重建成精确几何，圆就是真圆柱，再导出 STEP。\n\n用法就是把 .scad 文件或者整个项目文件夹拖进去，看一眼预览，点导出。不用安装，不用登录。Customizer 参数会变成滑块，先调好尺寸再导。\n\n它也有做不到的：`hull()` 和 `minkowski()` 对常见形状精确，碰到没有精确解的会近似并明确告诉你；带 `twist` 的 `linear_extrude` 没有精确形式；`import()` 进来的 STL 还是面片。这些都写在[兼容性](/zh/openscad-to-step/compatibility)那一页，我们用 Thingiverse 上几千个真实文件测过，大多数能直接转。\n\n## 怎么选\n\n- 只想要一个能打开的文件，不改：网格转 STEP 最快。\n- 要在 CAD 里继续改、要给加工厂、要 CAM：需要真正的 B-rep，用 FreeCAD 工作台或者我们的转换器。\n- 零件简单、一辈子只转一次：重画也行。\n\n如果你还没决定，先把文件拖进[转换器](/zh/openscad-to-step)看看。不行它会告诉你为什么。\n" },
];
